WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.230
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good morning, everybody.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.230
Welcome to this meeting in public of the Board
of NHS England.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.230
We’ve got various members of the public
in the audience with us today here in Skipton

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.870
House, so welcome to you, and also to the
vast hordes who are watching us online on

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.200
the live stream.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.980
As I always emphasise at this time, it’s
a meeting in public rather than a public meeting,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.930
and we have today a full board; no apologies
for absence.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:25.520
I would very much like to welcome Richard
Douglas to his first meeting of the Board.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.560
Richard is a member of the Board of NHS Improvement,
who will figure quite significantly in some

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.420
of the issues that we’ll be talking about
today.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:40.870
Richard, as you will recall, is a cross-appointment
to an associate membership of our Board, and

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:46.060
in the spirit of mutuality and reciprocity,
David Roberts is cross-appointed to the NHSI

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:52.230
Board, and this is a very important first
plank in the building of the new relationship

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.309
that we will be talking about more later on
today.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:00.590
There are no apologies for absence, as I have
said.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:05.610
Could I just please invite members of the
Board, if there are any declarations of interest

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.870
over and above those which we have on the
sheet of paper in front of you, but which

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.140
relate to particular items of business today.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.140
Wendy?

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:20.050
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: I guess, Malcolm, I just want
to officially state to the Board that since

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.150
our last meeting, I have become the Chairman
of Barnardo’s and have stepped down from

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:23.370
my role with the Prince’s Trust.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.370
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:28.790
We will record that, and it’s – of course
goes on the general register, but given the

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:33.350
focus today on health inequalities and other
issues, I think it’s important that that’s

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:34.350
reported.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.900
Okay, very much.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:42.350
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: And I’ve been appointed
as CEO of CR UK, but not started in that role.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.490
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.610
So, just to record Michelle’s appointment
as the new CEO of Cancer Research UK, but

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.450
with a start date still to come through.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.570
And the Board, by the way, would like to congratulate
both of you on those appointments which, in

00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:01.010
private consultations with you both, I’ve
come to the view that there is not an irresolvable

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:05.020
conflict of interest, albeit the interests
overlap, and there may come points when the

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.490
interests need to be redeclared.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.649
Okay, thank you very much.

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:17.049
We’ve got some presentations today, for
which we will be joined by Sarah-Jane Marsh,

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:22.980
by Neil Churchill and by Claire Murdoch, but
we’ll introduce them when we come to those

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.390
items on the agenda.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:33.590
There is no fire alarm due to be undertaken
today because we had it a quarter of an hour

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:38.030
ago and we’re still recovering, but the
test has been had, so that if the alarm does

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.799
go off, please, our staff will escort you
to a safe place out of the building.

00:02:44.799 --> 00:02:52.709
So, I wanted just to emphasise again that
this is a formal meeting of the Board, and

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:56.599
it’s undertaken in this way because of our
commitment that the public deserve to see

00:02:56.599 --> 00:03:01.709
a Board in deliberative mode and dealing with
issues of fundamental importance to the population

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:02.709
of the country.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:08.150
And at the same time, to stress that the business
of the Board is done in many different ways.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.780
It’s done through committees; we’ll have
a report from the various committees towards

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.319
the end of the meeting today, and quite substantive
work is done in those committees.

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:21.769
But that secondly, we also work through what
we call development sessions, where we work

00:03:21.769 --> 00:03:24.950
on early-stage strategy before formal papers
come to the Board.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:30.439
We use that as a unitary board: executive
members and non-executive members working

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:31.569
together.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:35.870
We did something unique yesterday, which was
to bring together this Board with the Board

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:42.499
of NHS Improvement, and to work through on
three critical areas of activity around our

00:03:42.499 --> 00:03:50.869
future working relationships, around financial
planning for ‘18/19, and around accountable

00:03:50.869 --> 00:03:53.360
– sorry, integrated care systems.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:57.659
But I mention those today because the papers
that came out of yesterday’s meeting will

00:03:57.659 --> 00:04:03.180
then come before a joint meeting of the two
boards that we will have in this room in public

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.510
session at two o’clock today.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:12.730
And there is a very significant earnest of
intention between these two boards to try

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:19.350
to surmount some of the obstacles towards
co-working at a national level not just as

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:24.160
a model for the co-working that we’re trying
to encourage at local level, but also, to

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.020
try to remove as many of the obstacles to
successful integration of care across the

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.760
country as we possibly can.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:38.480
So, let me then press on with the minutes
of the last meeting.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:44.090
Can I first of all invite the Board to let
me have any observations or comments, or corrections?

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.840
If not, then may I take them as an accurate
record?

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:47.840
Thank you.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.670
I have no matters arising.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.320
Do any of my colleagues have matters arising?

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.320
No.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.500
Okay, thank you very much.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.740
The next item is the Chairman’s’ Report.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:02.820
I’ve circulated a written note of some of
the activity that I’ve been involved in

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.880
since the last meeting.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.070
I would just highlight two further points.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:12.920
The first is that coming out of our last meeting,
you will have observed that on all of the

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:18.560
Board papers today, there is a heading of
patient and public involvement.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.850
That picks up from the report that we had
last time – thank you, Michelle and Victor

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:27.620
for your leadership of this – and we’re
now going to factor that in to all of our

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.120
Board reports.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:35.740
Secondly, to report that this will be the
last meeting to be attended by Sir John Burn,

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:40.470
whose tenure comes to an end at the end of
June.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:46.140
And I just have to say what a remarkable contribution,
John, you’ve made to the Board.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:52.920
Immensely appreciated, and I think your legacy
to the Board will continue long into the future.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.110
But thank you, again.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:55.660
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Thank you.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:06:00.790
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So, may I just ask the Board
if there are any questions, queries, comments,

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:04.400
or indeed criticisms of the Chairman’s Report?

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:10.480
If not, then I will move on very quickly – thank
you for that – and invite the Chief Executive

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:11.980
to present his report.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:12.980
Simon?

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.310
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, thanks very much, Malcolm.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:21.220
At the – by the time of our next meeting,
we will be in the middle of the 70th anniversary

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:28.710
of the National Health Service celebrations,
culminating on the 5th of July with the service

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.180
in Westminster Abbey.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:37.180
And it’s fair to say that in the run-up
to that, there’s a lot of public and media

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:42.050
and community attention on the brilliant work
that has been happening across the Health

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.050
Service.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:49.560
And one of the real positives in my job has
been the opportunity to meet with a number

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:55.740
of the award-winners drawn from across the
NHS in various of these national celebrations

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.200
over the last several weeks, in fact.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:05.960
The BBC One Show ran a fantastic series of
awards for people right across the country,

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.030
including volunteers and patients.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:16.300
ITV and the Mirror, with their NHS Heroes
campaign and celebration, was a fantastic

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:22.670
evening marking really amazing work often
under hugely difficult circumstances that

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.930
we’ve discussed many times around this table
in the past.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:31.020
The British Medical Journal had their annual
BMJ Awards from clinical teams drawn not just

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:35.770
from hospitals, but GP services, mental health
trusts, across the country, and earlier this

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.280
week I was at the London Ambulance Service
Awards, as well.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.610
So, it’s award season underway in the National
Health Service, and rightly so, as the whole

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:50.240
country actually reflects on what the National
Health Service is and its prospects for the

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.680
next decade.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.700
Those prospects are in part going to be influenced
by the things that we’re going to be talking

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:04.060
about at our joint meeting with NHS Improvement
later, but I would just underline a couple

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.830
of points that will be on our agenda there,
as well.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:15.170
The first is that we are quite clear that
pressing ahead with the – and accelerating

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:22.180
the move towards joined-up, integrated care
across the whole country for populations as

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:27.560
well as for individual patients is what futureproofing
the NHS is going to require.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:31.850
And so at that meeting later today, we will
have in front of us proposals to increase

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:37.110
to 12 million people the number of people
who are being covered by integrated care systems

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.450
across England.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:44.749
As we chart the next chapter in the history
of the NHS, looking out over the next five

00:08:44.749 --> 00:08:50.089
and ten years, we’re very keen that we do
that in an inclusive way, working with our

00:08:50.089 --> 00:08:56.190
national partners, with patients groups, clinicians,
the voluntary and community sector, as well

00:08:56.190 --> 00:09:02.850
as frontline leaders, and so we’ll have
before us a proposal to create an NHS Assembly,

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.959
which we will talk about more, that will draw
together from all parts of the country the

00:09:07.959 --> 00:09:13.330
diverse groups who are going to be needed
to help us chart that next direction.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:18.370
And underpinning all of that, of course, will
be ensuring that NHS England and NHS Improvement

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:26.930
as two organisations work together in a much
more aligned fashion, and we’ve got a very

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.020
fundamental set of proposals for us to consider
there.

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:35.230
So, that’s at our joint meeting, but I just
flag those because those are all of fundamental

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:41.779
importance to what we’ve got to get right
over the next 12 months, five and ten years.

00:09:41.779 --> 00:09:50.370
Perhaps he may not have the opportunity to
say it, but on our behalf, Steve Powis, National

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:56.100
Medical Director, went to Salisbury on Friday
afternoon to thank staff at Salisbury Hospital

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:02.889
who had dealt with the incident there, the
Skripals having been discharged, 65 people

00:10:02.889 --> 00:10:08.430
having been looked after as a result of that
incident.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:15.069
And not for now, but to also record that as
part of the wider discussion going on around

00:10:15.069 --> 00:10:19.839
the country’s future post-Brexit, the life
sciences sector is obviously a very important

00:10:19.839 --> 00:10:27.830
part of that, so a new Life Sciences Council
has been formed which I sit on with others,

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:33.300
and that has coincided with the kicking-off
of the new PPRS negotiation with the pharmaceutical

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.809
sector for the next five years.

00:10:35.809 --> 00:10:38.189
So, we are working on that.

00:10:38.189 --> 00:10:44.110
In a similar vein, sort of cross-sectorally,
we’ve been working with the Department of

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:53.259
Health and others on the childhood obesity
strategy mark II, which we hope to see shortly.

00:10:53.259 --> 00:11:01.769
And there’s been very good going on with
our partners at the Royal College of GPs and

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:07.689
the GPC to being to think through what a new
GP contract and the quality outcomes framework

00:11:07.689 --> 00:11:11.430
might be, that Ian and the team have been
leading, and we expect to be in a position

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:17.610
to move forward for consultation on that in
the near future, as well.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.519
So, wider work on things not otherwise covered
on the agenda here today.

00:11:21.519 --> 00:11:24.279
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, thank you, Simon.

00:11:24.279 --> 00:11:26.580
Any questions, queries, for Simon?

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:27.580
No.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:29.339
Thank you for that report.

00:11:29.339 --> 00:11:32.720
May we then move on, Jane, please, to the
Maternity Transformation Programme update?

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.499
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Thank you very much, Chair.

00:11:34.499 --> 00:11:38.160
Can I invite Sarah-Jane Marsh to come to the
Board?

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.940
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Thank you very much, Chairman.

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:40.940
Thank you.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:44.410
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: So, I – in the benefits
of time, and to – and I think to listen

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:49.019
to Sarah-Jane, I’m going to hand straight
over to her, but obviously, happy to join

00:11:49.019 --> 00:11:51.620
her in answering questions later.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:55.089
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Thank you, Jane, and thank
you to everybody for the opportunity to come

00:11:55.089 --> 00:11:59.279
and talk to you about this programme that
we feel massively passionate about, and has

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:04.149
actually been underway for two years now,
so we recently celebrated two years since

00:12:04.149 --> 00:12:05.899
the publication of Better Births.

00:12:05.899 --> 00:12:11.540
I’m sure people were very familiar with
it from the time, but in summary, the key

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:16.149
phrase we always use to everybody out in the
system, that Better Births is about making

00:12:16.149 --> 00:12:21.399
sure that every woman and family has a personal
and a safe experience of their birth.

00:12:21.399 --> 00:12:25.610
That doesn’t matter whether they’re at
home having a normal birth, or whether they’re

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:29.199
in an operating theatre having an elective
caesarean section.

00:12:29.199 --> 00:12:34.639
We can give everybody a personal and safe
experience if we get our care right, and that’s

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:37.569
what we’ve been working really hard on over
the last two years.

00:12:37.569 --> 00:12:42.490
So, the first thing I wanted to share with
you is that we are demonstrating that this

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:44.889
can be done right across the country.

00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:49.779
We have a range of early adopters and pioneers
who’ve been working away and have achieved

00:12:49.779 --> 00:12:50.779
some great things.

00:12:50.779 --> 00:12:54.980
And just to share some highlights, one of
the key parts of Better Births was that more

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:59.420
care would take place in the community, in
community hubs, not just to keep women away

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:05.129
from hospital in their local areas, but also
to think about how that care is integrated

00:13:05.129 --> 00:13:08.540
with the wider services that we know that
women and families need.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.720
So, some of our community hubs, for example,
are co-located in children’s centres, so

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.529
we’re not just giving that mother the care
closer to home from the outset, we’re giving

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:21.259
them opportunity for the child to get the
very best start in life.

00:13:21.259 --> 00:13:25.750
Many of our early adopters have been really
focusing on making sure as well that those

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:30.420
community hubs are in areas that are perhaps
more difficult to reach, so less affluent

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:35.600
areas, isolated communities, and really helping
women to create peer networks for once their

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.660
baby comes along, to give the baby the best
start in life.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:43.850
We’re also very much underway with our continuity
of care, which is probably our flagship scheme

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:44.850
from Better Births.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:50.069
This is ensuring that every woman has an opportunity
to have continuity from the team looking after

00:13:50.069 --> 00:13:54.529
them, to have a named midwife that’s in
a group of between about four and six, but

00:13:54.529 --> 00:13:58.970
in the best-case scenario, would see them
antenatally, postnatally and for the birth

00:13:58.970 --> 00:13:59.970
itself.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:03.380
And again, we’ve got areas right across
the country now that are starting to implement

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:04.380
this.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:08.170
We’ve got schemes going on in – across
40% of the country, and they’re all different

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:09.170
in different ways.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:13.879
But again, I just wanted to reassure people
that this is not about white middle-class

00:14:13.879 --> 00:14:18.779
women with very articulate views getting the
continuity and the choices they want: this

00:14:18.779 --> 00:14:19.800
is about everybody.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.759
And many of our continuity of carer leaders
are absolutely focusing on those women that

00:14:24.759 --> 00:14:29.519
they feel are the most vulnerable, so those
with complex social needs, teenage mothers,

00:14:29.519 --> 00:14:33.519
women with learning disabilities; they’ve
really been prioritised to make sure that

00:14:33.519 --> 00:14:34.910
they get continuity.

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:37.120
We know that gives a better experience.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:42.550
As importantly, it also gives a better outcome
in terms of reduction in stillbirths, etc.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:46.829
We’ve also smashed through our personalised
maternity care budget target.

00:14:46.829 --> 00:14:52.319
We set ourselves a target of achieving 10,000
personalised budgets by the end of March,

00:14:52.319 --> 00:14:56.410
and we’re now on 19,000 across our seven
pilot sites.

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:59.860
We’ve got a lot of learning from that: we
deliberately weren’t prescriptive about

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:02.740
the way it was done, and areas have done it
in different ways.

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:08.380
But we are starting to see some advantages
come from that, not least widening choice,

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:13.860
partly widening choice into the independent
sector with independent midwives, but also

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:18.529
just making it really clear what existing
NHS choices are available in particular areas

00:15:18.529 --> 00:15:19.589
of the country just wasn’t known before.

00:15:19.589 --> 00:15:23.540
In some instances, women were just going to
their local hospitals because that was the

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.260
only choice they thought that they had.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:30.019
So, that is working; we’re just evaluating
that to decide whether or not we should take

00:15:30.019 --> 00:15:31.500
it to a national roll-out.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:35.600
Building on the theme of the Chief Executive’s
introduction, we’ve been really working

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:42.189
hard as a national team to support the frontline
leaders to be able to do this to delivery,

00:15:42.189 --> 00:15:46.899
and the board that leads this is literally
a one of everybody, so we’ve got NHS England,

00:15:46.899 --> 00:15:51.389
NHS Improvement, Health Education England,
the CQC, Public Health England, the Royal

00:15:51.389 --> 00:15:54.499
College of Obstetrics and Gynaecologists,
the RCN.

00:15:54.499 --> 00:15:58.569
We’re all working together to see what it
is that we can do to enable the change to

00:15:58.569 --> 00:16:00.160
happen further and faster.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:01.639
Just a few examples.

00:16:01.639 --> 00:16:06.610
We’ve got a safety collaborative now up
and running, which NHS Improvement are leading

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:07.610
on.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:11.410
Two thirds of our maternity units now right
across the country have taken part in this

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:14.769
safety collaborative, where they’ve all
– they’re all taking the key things in

00:16:14.769 --> 00:16:19.820
their local units that are a problem for them,
and, using the quality improvement technique

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:23.399
from the IHI, are starting to implement a
range of improvement measures.

00:16:23.399 --> 00:16:27.519
We’ve got the Healthcare Safety Investigation
Branch, which means all of our serious incidents

00:16:27.519 --> 00:16:32.370
in maternity now will have an independent
investigation through a standardised and rigorous

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:33.370
approach.

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:37.060
At the end of that, we’ll be able to understand
better the key themes of why things go wrong

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:42.850
in maternity, but more importantly than that,
we’re learning how to explore what we already

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:47.230
know about safety in a new way by taking and
walking our teams through this, so everybody

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:50.649
really understands and internalises things
that have gone wrong.

00:16:50.649 --> 00:16:56.420
And on electronic maternity record, we are
in the process of improvement and widening

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:59.329
access for women to be able to access their
personal record.

00:16:59.329 --> 00:17:03.660
Currently, that’s only possible for about
1,000 women across the country, and we have

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:07.470
a programme to get to 150,000 by the end of
the year.

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:11.350
I’m not as confident I can smash through
that target as the personalised budget one,

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:15.439
but we will do our very best to ensure that
more women can get access to their personal

00:17:15.439 --> 00:17:16.439
record.

00:17:16.439 --> 00:17:20.980
And we’ve also had the second wave of our
specialist perinatal mental health teams,

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:26.160
which means we’ve almost now got full coverage
across the country for women with moderate

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:30.070
to severe mental health conditions, to make
sure they’ve got a specialist team that’s

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:34.289
able to provide care to them throughout the
first year of their baby’s life.

00:17:34.289 --> 00:17:37.870
We’ve also got everything in place that
we need for full national roll-out; every

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:41.160
single STP area has a local maternity system.

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:45.980
Each of those local maternity systems has
a leader, and we know who those leaders are.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:50.540
And we bring them together every six months
to talk about what the issues and challenges

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:53.950
are, what progress they’re making, and what
more we can do to help and support.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:58.470
Everyone’s got a plan, as I say, and we’ve
really created a sense of a leadership community

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:00.230
there, where everybody knows what the job
is.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:04.470
This cannot possibly be a thing that only
happens in areas where people are enthusiastic.

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:08.549
We’re absolutely determined, particularly
through the NHS England team, that every woman

00:18:08.549 --> 00:18:12.470
benefits from this programme right across
the country.

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:17.230
Then just by way of conclusion, I guess, we’ve
obviously got challenges and risks just like

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:18.700
any programme might have.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:26.020
No matter what progress we make across our
safety agenda, we are all – take very seriously

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:29.570
any safety concerns, and we know there’s
a range of maternity services across the country

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:32.399
that are rated inadequate by the CQC.

00:18:32.399 --> 00:18:36.090
And one of the things that’s very much in
our sights is how we can work alongside them,

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:40.010
using our programmes and techniques, to help
and support, and we have actually managed

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:45.030
to help moving a couple of maternity units
out of inadequate and – from the CQC’s

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.640
list over the last year.

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:52.390
We have made a range of successes across many
of the areas that we are measuring.

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:58.200
So, a slight improvement around smoking cessation
and breastfeeding rates, and stillbirths,

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:02.140
but one of the areas that we’ve not made
the progress we wanted is around death in

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:03.450
the neonatal period.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:07.360
We’re just doing a lot more work on that
at our board at the moment to understand exactly

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:11.940
what that relates to, but it’s happened
for two years in a row now of data that’s

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.150
available to us; it goes back to 2016.

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:17.640
So, we’re exploring that in a lot more detail,
including with the people that are leading

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:22.090
the neonatal strategic review, to see what
some of the more strategic issues might be.

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:26.170
And just like every programme, we have workforce
challenges.

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:29.710
We very much welcome the announcement of the
additional midwives, which we worked very

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:33.130
closely on with the Department of Health,
and that will make a massive difference.

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:37.070
But nevertheless, in the here and now, we
know many maternity units are under lots of

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:40.760
pressure, not just with midwifery shortages,
but also with issues with the middle-grade

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:41.760
rota.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:46.419
So, just by way of conclusion, we have absolutely
amazing staff across our maternity services

00:19:46.419 --> 00:19:47.419
in the NHS.

00:19:47.419 --> 00:19:48.419
Truly amazing.

00:19:48.419 --> 00:19:52.690
And they’ve taken every opportunity that
we’ve given them, through the national programme

00:19:52.690 --> 00:19:57.010
and with the national transformation funding,
to make the very best of this they can.

00:19:57.010 --> 00:20:01.680
They are supported by some of the most engaged
women and service users.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:05.269
You only have to ask anybody in maternity
to come and help and support, get involved

00:20:05.269 --> 00:20:09.190
in everything, and everybody comes running,
so this is women designing the services that

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:12.500
they want for themselves, and it’s truly
inspiring.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:16.190
And also, I’ve personally been supported
in my role by a simply amazing team at NHS

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:17.190
England.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.670
There are some wonderful people who work here
who’ve absolutely helped and enabled these

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:24.770
people on the frontline to make this happen,
so I think we feel very proud of the progress

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:29.340
that we’ve made, but we are very much two
years in to what we see as a five-year programme,

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.289
and we’ve still got further to go to.

00:20:32.289 --> 00:20:33.289
Thank you.

00:20:33.289 --> 00:20:34.990
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for that
presentation, and also for your leadership

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:35.990
of the programme.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:38.360
Can I just – I’ve got a question that’s
come straight out of that presentation.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:41.090
I mean, the thing that really concerns me
is neonatal death.

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:42.090
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yes.

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:44.830
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So, we see a reduction in
the figures about stillbirths –

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:45.830
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Stillbirths.

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:49.820
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: – coming down, but we’re
aiming at 50% reduction in all of these causes

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:56.740
of death, and I still am very troubled that
we are out of line with other leading European

00:20:56.740 --> 00:21:03.090
countries, and yet we’re still not fully
understanding what might be the contributing

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:06.900
factors in neonatal death.

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:10.510
And as you said, it’s been at a constant
figure over the last two years rather than

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.410
starting to approach the 50% reduction that
we’d like to see.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:17.790
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: So, we are getting much
better at investigating all deaths and understanding

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:22.130
the root causes of what’s happened, and
what common themes that we need to address.

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:27.620
I think that Embrace do a fantastic report
each year, which themes all these things together,

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:31.269
and looks at whether it’s related to, for
example, that there’s more opportunities

00:21:31.269 --> 00:21:35.990
now to keep babies alive, and therefore, is
that having an impact, you know, overall?

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:37.960
So –
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Is that data collected on

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:41.250
a consistent basis, and comparisons with other
nations?

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:46.220
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yes, it is, though as
in all of these examples, our data in the

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:52.030
NHS appears to be far more robust and far-reaching
than in other countries, and therefore, I’m

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:55.450
not entirely sure we’re always comparing
things against each other.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:59.570
That’s why, in our programme, we’re very
clear to compare progress against ourselves

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:03.480
and see whether our own interventions are
actually improving and making things better.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:08.130
But as I say, the Embrace report – I mean,
it goes back to 2016, so one of the issues

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:12.450
that we’ve always got is, we’ve done a
lot of work over the last year, and we don’t

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:16.610
yet have the data to know whether the work
that we’ve done has made an impact or not.

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:19.779
I guess the reassurance I would give to you,
Chairman, is that we’ve made this one of

00:22:19.779 --> 00:22:22.450
our priority areas as a board, and we’re
going to get underneath it; we’ve actually

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:25.270
got a big session on it at our next board
in June.

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:27.970
We’re going to try and get underneath this
more.

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:32.760
I do think there are some strategic issues
as well, to do with strategic commissioning.

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:38.340
In an ideal world, our neonatal cots would
be configured differently to what they are,

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:43.100
but we have challenges about where the neonatal
cots are compared to where the maternity units

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:49.350
are, and we know that many women don’t actually
give birth in the place they ideally would

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:54.080
need to, to get access to the intensive care
cot, and there’s a piece of work that’s

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:59.230
being led by Professor Neil Marlow that’s
looking at this, to say, have we – are there

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:01.590
more – some strategic solutions, as well?

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:06.380
Do we need to cluster more of our level-three
neonatal care together, and ensure that women

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:11.019
that need these high-end services get access
to them in a timely way, and we’re not moving

00:23:11.019 --> 00:23:16.179
our babies round our systems in ambulances,
which we are at the moment sometimes, because

00:23:16.179 --> 00:23:18.409
the cots aren’t necessarily where we need
them to be?

00:23:18.409 --> 00:23:21.309
So, I think we’ve got enough, but they’re
not always in the right places.

00:23:21.309 --> 00:23:24.269
But that’s very much on our sort of overall
plan, as well.

00:23:24.269 --> 00:23:29.130
What we don’t want is a top-down NHS England
solution that says, ‘Put them all here’.

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:33.760
It’s for the LMSs to take the data that’s
available and start to work through a plan

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.390
that makes sense in their local area.

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:37.130
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, thanks very much.

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:38.130
John?

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:42.510
&gt;&gt;John Burn: So, Jane, thank you for your
energy and enthusiasm, which is tremendous.

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:44.779
You mentioned briefly there smoking in pregnancy.

00:23:44.779 --> 00:23:45.779
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yes.

00:23:45.779 --> 00:23:49.410
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Just to highlight, my colleague
Eugene Milne who’s the Director of Public

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:54.580
Health in Newcastle showed me some very impressive
data where the investment in persuading women

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:59.580
not to smoke in pregnancy was associated with
birth weights moving towards the mean.

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:00.580
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Absolutely.

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:04.519
&gt;&gt;John Burn: And intriguingly, to come back
to Malcolm’s point, the neonatal death rate

00:24:04.519 --> 00:24:10.190
was showing a similar improvement: too small
to prove statistically, but it would be in

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:12.440
keeping with that.

00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:17.139
Eugene was anxious about the investment in
that programme, and I know that’s on the

00:24:17.139 --> 00:24:19.630
side of local councils and Public Health England.

00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:23.990
Is there anything we can do to continue to
help with that programme?

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:26.000
Because it’s clearly a very important intervention.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:27.230
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yeah.

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:32.679
So, one of the key messages that we always
give out in all of our sort of local contact

00:24:32.679 --> 00:24:36.429
is, it’s far better to invest in smoking
cessation than neonatal cots.

00:24:36.429 --> 00:24:39.730
And somehow, we’ve got ourselves into a
mess where the funding streams for the two

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:43.889
things aren’t in the same place, and so
therefore, we end up doing not always the

00:24:43.889 --> 00:24:44.889
right things.

00:24:44.889 --> 00:24:49.210
Now, our approach has been to say that public
health needs to be absolutely embedded in

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:53.470
all of our local maternity systems, and in
an ideal world, that’s a very engaged and

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:55.480
informed local authority.

00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:59.191
However, where there are issues – and we
know there are issues with local authority

00:24:59.191 --> 00:25:04.049
funding – we have to think very carefully
as an NHS about what we’re doing with our

00:25:04.049 --> 00:25:05.049
money.

00:25:05.049 --> 00:25:08.159
So, we’ve got some really good examples
in the country where maternity support workers

00:25:08.159 --> 00:25:13.390
are being used to do smoking cessation, and
actually, where you embed the maternity support

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:18.120
worker in the continuity team I referred to,
they can get just as good outcomes for women.

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:19.690
So, I think it’s a both/and.

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:24.690
I mean, anything that anybody can do to advocate
for more smoking cessation, not just in pregnancy,

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:28.639
but everywhere, because it’s definitely
not what it used to be, would be very welcome.

00:25:28.639 --> 00:25:33.019
But I think in maternity, we feel we’ve
got to keep what we control, as well, and

00:25:33.019 --> 00:25:37.639
that’s absolutely sort of holding up these
examples, really, that it’s not just local

00:25:37.639 --> 00:25:42.789
authority smoking cessation that can do smoking
cessation well, but – yeah, it’s a very

00:25:42.789 --> 00:25:44.269
serious point.

00:25:44.269 --> 00:25:47.210
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Thank you.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:53.690
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, I’ve never known
the Board actually lost for words.

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:57.820
Moira, I’m glad you’re going to save us.

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:03.700
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Yes, again, very positive, and
well done to everyone involved.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:07.720
But I just wondered, two thirds had taken
part in the safety collaborative.

00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:08.720
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yes.

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:12.010
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Is the final third about to
do that, or is there resistance?

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:15.330
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yeah – it’s not resistance,
it’s just that we’ve only got the capacity

00:26:15.330 --> 00:26:16.419
to do it in three waves.

00:26:16.419 --> 00:26:20.279
So, we had wave one last year, we’ve just
launched a wave two, and then wave three’s

00:26:20.279 --> 00:26:21.350
coming down the tracks.

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:25.700
And actually, one of the debates that we did
have was, do we just go with the enthusiastic

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:29.490
people who put their hands up first, or is
there actually some assessment between those

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.970
who need to be in a safety collaborative and
those that are prioritised?

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:36.269
And we actually managed to achieve a balance
of the two, so you always get the ones that

00:26:36.269 --> 00:26:41.669
you need to use as the exemplars, but other
people did come along and join in, as well.

00:26:41.669 --> 00:26:44.279
So, yes, there will be full coverage next
year: everybody will have been through the

00:26:44.279 --> 00:26:45.279
collaborative, definitely.

00:26:45.279 --> 00:26:46.279
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:26:46.279 --> 00:26:47.279
Joanne and Stephen.

00:26:47.279 --> 00:26:48.279
Joanne?

00:26:48.279 --> 00:26:55.390
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I wanted to ask you what you
saw as the role of technology in helping women

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:59.980
sort of manage their own pregnancies and contributing
to the fantastic outcomes that you’re looking

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:00.980
for.

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:04.179
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yes, so when the consultation
was done around Better Births, I think lots

00:27:04.179 --> 00:27:08.950
of women that were consulted with were absolutely
clear they wanted to be able to take more

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:12.549
control and have a better understanding of
their pregnancy journey, and we’ve done

00:27:12.549 --> 00:27:14.149
a lot of work on this as a programme.

00:27:14.149 --> 00:27:18.259
So, from the sort of simple – I’m not
belittling them, but you know, the straightforward

00:27:18.259 --> 00:27:21.750
apps that we know women really appreciate,
so we’ve got one of our apps on the apps

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.820
library now is a counter that women can – the
baby kicks counter, which we know can be a

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:27.899
big driver in the reduction in stillbirth.

00:27:27.899 --> 00:27:30.679
So, we’ve got those kinds of examples that
are working well.

00:27:30.679 --> 00:27:35.290
I think the sort of panacea for us is that
a women would have access to her own maternity

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:39.400
record on a smartphone, and this is what we’re
working on at the moment to try – because

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:43.009
at the moment, that is really just a handful
of women that have got access, and that’s

00:27:43.009 --> 00:27:47.880
our big sort of target that we will have a
quarter of the country covered, or a quarter

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:53.289
of the women that are giving birth covered
in due course, because I think it’s – pregnancy

00:27:53.289 --> 00:27:57.980
or maternity is different to many other NHS
services in that, you know, historically,

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:02.039
women have always carried their notes around
with them anyway; they fully own them and,

00:28:02.039 --> 00:28:06.200
you know, we can’t take that away from them
as we move to a technology solution.

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:11.980
And people really want to own their own pregnancy
journeys, so I think technology plays a very

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:13.250
significant role.

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:17.680
It also plays a role in joined-up care and
choice for professionals as well, because

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:22.340
one of the things that we’re starting to
get better at is moving women around the maternity

00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:24.710
system to where the right care is for them.

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:29.840
So, if one particular unit is full, it might
be then that another unit can help out or

00:28:29.840 --> 00:28:34.539
support, or a woman might have started thinking
that she was on a high-risk pathway, and then

00:28:34.539 --> 00:28:36.370
she can be on a low-risk pathway.

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:39.720
We need the technology to move between the
healthcare professionals, as well, and we

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:43.799
see those two things as interrelated, really,
that you’ve got the connectivity between

00:28:43.799 --> 00:28:47.280
professionals and you’ve got the connectivity
between the professionals and the women.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:49.760
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Thank you very much.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:55.070
So, firstly, can I echo John’s comments
about the personal enthusiasm that you bring

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:58.629
to the programme, which is manifest.

00:28:58.629 --> 00:29:03.009
You will notice that later on the agenda,
we have a paper which describes the work that

00:29:03.009 --> 00:29:06.899
we’re doing in our key programmes on – to
reduce health inequalities, and I know you

00:29:06.899 --> 00:29:11.259
mentioned that in your presentation, but I
wonder, seeing we have you here, whether you

00:29:11.259 --> 00:29:15.090
might want to take the opportunity to say
a little bit more about the work that’s

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:16.149
specifically targeting health inequalities.

00:29:16.149 --> 00:29:19.629
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yeah, I mean, I think
it’s one of the things that we were concerned

00:29:19.629 --> 00:29:23.050
about at the beginning, that it didn’t become
a programme that was about the vocal and the

00:29:23.050 --> 00:29:26.700
people who knew how to access the choice and
the information, and – you know, all – so

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:30.000
it’s literally threaded throughout absolutely
everything we do.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:34.110
So, the maternity care budget was an example
where we said, ‘Right, this has got to be

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:35.610
equal access for anybody’.

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:41.309
So, for example, if it’s somebody that we
know is going to have additional needs, when

00:29:41.309 --> 00:29:44.179
they’re having their appointments, they
get a double slot booked for them, so the

00:29:44.179 --> 00:29:47.440
midwife has got more time to be able to talk
to them and talk them through their choice

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:48.720
options.

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:52.970
As I say, most of our continuity pilots have
absolutely prioritised those women that they

00:29:52.970 --> 00:30:00.419
feel can benefit from that continuity: those
with more social needs, as I’ve mentioned,

00:30:00.419 --> 00:30:02.450
those with learning disabilities, etc.

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:07.420
What we’re also finding in those pilots
is that whilst we’ve not necessarily thought

00:30:07.420 --> 00:30:11.409
this was the area that we’re targeting,
when you’ve got continuity, more women are

00:30:11.409 --> 00:30:16.140
starting to disclose other things about their
life, because they’ve build a relationship

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:19.990
with the professional, where we actually realise
they were more vulnerable than when we started,

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:25.049
and I think this is a very powerful thing
that’s come through for us, and as I say,

00:30:25.049 --> 00:30:27.690
midwives have really got behind that.

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:31.200
The community hubs, not only have the – a
lot of thought about where they physically

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:35.480
are, so they’re not in the usual places,
trying to get them sort of out into other

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:41.250
parts of the – other parts of areas and
systems, and getting them embedded in communities.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:44.529
Because one of the big risks – and I think
we’re very conscious of this in our programme

00:30:44.529 --> 00:30:46.629
– is the postnatal period.

00:30:46.629 --> 00:30:50.799
Maternity care can sort of tail off, and then
women feel very much on their own and isolated.

00:30:50.799 --> 00:30:55.110
So, if we can get them connected with their
local community and the community services

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:59.970
and the early years offer that children centres
have, then that’s a massive benefit.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:04.450
Again, the smoking cessation we’ve talked
about, and making sure that we know the sort

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:08.660
of overall demographic of people that are
more likely to smoke in pregnancy, and making

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:14.530
sure that we, where possible, increase our
access there and give bespoke support, culturally

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:16.629
sensitive support around breastfeeding.

00:31:16.629 --> 00:31:21.990
So, I think our approach of making sure health
inequalities isn’t a workstream of our programme,

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:26.490
but something that every programme in our
workstream has to demonstrate they are meeting,

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:30.289
has been probably one of the massive advantages
of what we’ve done, and actually, midwives

00:31:30.289 --> 00:31:35.110
are one of the groups who absolutely get this,
because they see the consequences.

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:38.549
They know that stillbirth is related to inequality.

00:31:38.549 --> 00:31:40.850
They see it with their own eyes every day.

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:43.970
So, we didn’t have to convince anybody;
straightaway, they were saying, ‘Right,

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:47.750
the traveller group: we’ve got to prioritise
the traveller groups’, or, you know, whatever.

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:48.750
So – yeah.

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:49.870
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Can I just follow up with
a –

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:50.870
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Please.

00:31:50.870 --> 00:31:54.390
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Are you confident that you
have the specific measures in place that allow

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:58.120
you to track the differences that you are
going – you anticipate you’ll make in

00:31:58.120 --> 00:31:59.120
inequalities?

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:00.120
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Yes.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:03.870
So, I am now, but we’ve only just really
got our baseline data over the last two or

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:04.870
three months.

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:09.600
So, I think the next sort of one to two years
is going to be really crucial in terms of,

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:11.529
we’re confident that this is making a difference.

00:32:11.529 --> 00:32:14.460
But we have, yes, got a range of measures
in place now that we’re going to be able

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:16.669
to track as an overall board.

00:32:16.669 --> 00:32:23.429
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Can I just ask, one of the
things that you refer to in the paper is the

00:32:23.429 --> 00:32:26.820
role of the stakeholders group chaired by
Baroness Cumberlege, and of course, this arises

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:28.860
out of the original report –
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: That’s correct.

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:30.179
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: – that she led on.

00:32:30.179 --> 00:32:33.169
Was that of assistance to you in the health
inequalities front?

00:32:33.169 --> 00:32:39.029
Do you have users who can help to represent
some of the interests of those with health

00:32:39.029 --> 00:32:40.029
inequalities?

00:32:40.029 --> 00:32:42.910
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: So, the Stakeholder Council
is a huge force for good, I think, throughout

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:44.799
the entire programme.

00:32:44.799 --> 00:32:48.539
The council that Julia chairs, it has got
all those stakeholders on, and their role

00:32:48.539 --> 00:32:53.480
is to hold us to account, to make sure that
we stay true to the vision in Better Births.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:57.179
And every meeting, Baroness Cumberlege goes
round and gets updates from everybody, ‘Does

00:32:57.179 --> 00:32:58.820
it feel like this in your local area?

00:32:58.820 --> 00:32:59.970
Is it actually working?

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:01.120
What are the women saying?

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:05.370
What are the maternity voice partnerships
which are part of each one of our systems,

00:33:05.370 --> 00:33:06.570
what are they saying?’

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:08.370
And every board meeting, we discuss that.

00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:11.690
So, we don’t ever get ourselves into a false
sense of security that because something’s

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:14.900
green on a piece of paper, it feels like it’s
green out there.

00:33:14.900 --> 00:33:19.700
So, I really do think having the Stakeholder
Council’s been really powerful, and sort

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:23.730
of presentationally, we’ve been very keen
to display that the two things are side by

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:24.730
side.

00:33:24.730 --> 00:33:28.639
This isn’t about having a group under here
that sort of reports up and down; this is

00:33:28.639 --> 00:33:33.999
about two groups working together using their
different skills to ensure that we do the

00:33:33.999 --> 00:33:35.320
very, very best we can.

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:36.320
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Great.

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:37.629
Thank you for that.

00:33:37.629 --> 00:33:41.400
I’m conscious also that perinatal mental
health was a big issue, but fortunately, we’ve

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:45.379
got Claire Murdoch who’s going to come and
introduce the next paper.

00:33:45.379 --> 00:33:49.260
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: She’s equally as passionate
about it as I am, so – yeah.

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:52.269
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So, well, thank you very
much for that presentation.

00:33:52.269 --> 00:33:53.269
Simon?

00:33:53.269 --> 00:33:55.870
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: One of the – also, to thank
Matt Tagney, who works with Sarah-Jane, has

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:58.809
done a lot of the heavy lifting on the programme,
as well.

00:33:58.809 --> 00:33:59.809
So, thank you, Matt.

00:33:59.809 --> 00:34:04.450
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, then I think we repeat
our thanks both to Matt, but to Sarah-Jane

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:05.450
for your leadership.

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:07.519
It’s what actually makes these transformations
happen.

00:34:07.519 --> 00:34:12.179
But by the way, we’re following it really
closely, and look forward to further updates.

00:34:12.179 --> 00:34:13.179
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Okay.

00:34:13.179 --> 00:34:14.179
Well, happy to return at any time you’d
like.

00:34:14.179 --> 00:34:15.179
Thank you very much.

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:16.179
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, thank you very much.

00:34:16.179 --> 00:34:17.179
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Thank you.

00:34:17.179 --> 00:34:21.010
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we then move on then,
please, to the mental health programme update.

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:23.720
And delighted to welcome Claire Murdoch, who’s
the SRO.

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:27.079
Claire, I think you last did a presentation
to us about a year ago.

00:34:27.079 --> 00:34:28.200
Is that –
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Possibly, yes.

00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:29.200
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Welcome back.

00:34:29.200 --> 00:34:30.200
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you.

00:34:30.200 --> 00:34:31.200
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Hey.

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:32.200
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: It’s good to be back.

00:34:32.200 --> 00:34:33.260
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: No, it’s fantastic.

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:37.460
And there is just so much activity for you
to report on.

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:42.659
So, is – Jane, are you going to say anything,
or Steve, before I introduce Claire to go

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:44.119
straight ahead?

00:34:44.119 --> 00:34:48.110
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: That’d be a no.

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:54.089
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, Matthew, would you
be happy to hand straight over to Claire?

00:34:54.089 --> 00:34:55.200
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you, Chair.

00:34:55.200 --> 00:34:56.290
That seems like a good idea.

00:34:56.290 --> 00:34:57.740
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, right, we’re in agreement
on that one.

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:00.130
Right, Claire, you’re off.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:02.520
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you.

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:10.069
So, it’s really good to be back today, and
ostensibly be reporting on the two-year progress

00:35:10.069 --> 00:35:12.740
of a five-year programme.

00:35:12.740 --> 00:35:18.220
Before I do, I want to say a huge thank you
to the NHS England Board and my colleagues

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:25.890
here for giving this real prominence and priority,
and out into the regions and STPs and provider

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:31.740
organisations right across the country, because
one of the first things I have noted, in the

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:37.440
two years I’ve been privileged to undertake
this role, is the extent to which we have

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:44.550
energised the country around the art and science
of the possible in terms of greater ambition

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:47.680
and access for mental health services in England.

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:52.520
You’ve got a fairly granular detail paper
here.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:56.390
I’m not going to talk you all the way through
it, but I would just like to, if I might,

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:59.180
remind people of the context that we’re
operating in.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:05.010
So, we know the Five Year Forward View for
mental health, we published our action plan

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:08.160
in July 2016.

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:14.259
And it came about as a result of the independent
task force, which spoke to 20,000 people nationally

00:36:14.259 --> 00:36:19.359
– professionals, politicians, the public,
patients – about what was most important.

00:36:19.359 --> 00:36:25.510
And out of those recommendations – out of
that report, rather, fell 58 recommendations

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:30.369
for better mental health services and the
improvement of mental health services in England.

00:36:30.369 --> 00:36:35.820
Approximately half of those 58 recommendations
were for the NHS, and the other half were

00:36:35.820 --> 00:36:41.370
for wider society, because I think we all
need to acknowledge two things, big things,

00:36:41.370 --> 00:36:44.930
about mental health in the context within
which our programme lands.

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:49.369
Firstly, it’s not just an NHS responsibility.

00:36:49.369 --> 00:36:56.020
Mental health can begin in schools, in healthy
and resilient societies or not, deprivation

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:59.650
can be a big cause, housing, employment, and
so on and so forth.

00:36:59.650 --> 00:37:03.119
We need our other partners at that table with
us.

00:37:03.119 --> 00:37:09.040
And the second thing is that this is the first,
not the last, step, I would argue, and I think

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:14.530
other colleagues would agree, in a longer
transformation of mental health services in

00:37:14.530 --> 00:37:15.530
this country.

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:19.330
So, we know that much of what we’re trying
to achieve – for example, for children and

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:23.859
young people – is against quite a low starting
point.

00:37:23.859 --> 00:37:28.960
We think we see one in four children in specialist
CAMHS at the moment who need it.

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:31.520
And by the end of this programme, we’ll
see one in three.

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:36.670
So, I am going to focus on what we’ve achieved
thus far, but I think those two factors are

00:37:36.670 --> 00:37:41.970
really important to hold in mind, because
whilst we celebrate progress, we’ve got

00:37:41.970 --> 00:37:45.099
much further to do.

00:37:45.099 --> 00:37:52.099
So, the half of the recommendations that were
for the NHS set out what I would call a muscular,

00:37:52.099 --> 00:38:01.490
very clear plan of action to improve access
and the – both access to services and the

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.250
amount of people seen across the age range.

00:38:04.250 --> 00:38:10.569
So, literally, children and young people through
to adults of working age, and older people.

00:38:10.569 --> 00:38:15.579
I think it probably is one of our most ambitious
programmes.

00:38:15.579 --> 00:38:20.099
Mental health isn’t just a thing; it’s
many diagnoses, many treatments, many sorts

00:38:20.099 --> 00:38:26.180
of services, just as physical health is many
sorts of interventions, and so on and so forth.

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:32.490
What you see in my report, page two and three
of my report and a bit longer, is progress

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:36.510
we’ve made against a myriad of service developments.

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:41.579
I’d like to draw your eye to a couple of
highlights, but I want to use a – steal

00:38:41.579 --> 00:38:42.970
a word from Sarah-Jane, actually.

00:38:42.970 --> 00:38:49.780
I think we’ve smashed some of our targets,
and we’ve listed what they are there.

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:53.849
But I think some particular highlights in
the last two years, and certainly since I

00:38:53.849 --> 00:38:58.780
last was here, was, for example, the spread
of the perinatal services.

00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:04.430
We set up wave one, and we’ve just established
wave two – we’ve just announced wave two

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:07.180
– of specialist perinatal services across
the country.

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:14.470
When we began, something like 4% of the country
had access to specialist perinatal services.

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:19.170
When we finish, complete this programme in
2021, the whole country will have access;

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:23.670
wave two now consolidates that presence in
all parts of the country.

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:28.470
In year one, we hoped we’d see 2,000 women
through that programme, because we were having

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:31.540
to recruit, establish the expertise, and so
on and so forth.

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:34.710
We’ve actually seen closer, we think, to
7,000 women.

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:40.480
And by the end of the programme, we’ll have
seen 30,000.

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:45.440
And that’s a great example of – I was
delighted when Sarah-Jane mentioned it, because

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:51.011
one of our big themes is integration of physical
and mental health, not creating our mental

00:39:51.011 --> 00:39:57.059
health programme in isolation from, in this
case, midwives, health visitors, the GP, the

00:39:57.059 --> 00:39:58.730
acute hospital.

00:39:58.730 --> 00:40:01.099
Another highlight has got to be IAPT.

00:40:01.099 --> 00:40:06.770
We’ve – this is Increasing Access to Psychological
Therapies, a catchy title.

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:11.670
But we think we run the biggest evidence-based
talking therapy programme for people with

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:17.400
common mental health problems in the entire
world, actually, is what we think, and certainly,

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:23.000
other countries are now copying us: Canada,
Denmark, Israel, Australia.

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:27.079
We were written about in the New York Times
this last year.

00:40:27.079 --> 00:40:31.480
The problem with common mental health problems
is it gives the impression that they’re

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:33.310
not debilitating or serious.

00:40:33.310 --> 00:40:34.700
They can be, and they are.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:36.510
So, this programme’s incredibly important.

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:44.619
We’ve said we’ll see an additional 600,000
people in talking therapies by 2021, and this

00:40:44.619 --> 00:40:51.599
year – every year, we have very clear trajectories
towards that extra 600,000, and we’ve smashed

00:40:51.599 --> 00:40:52.599
that.

00:40:52.599 --> 00:40:56.980
But we don’t only measure access in that
programme; we also measure outcomes in terms

00:40:56.980 --> 00:41:01.530
of recovery rates, and we’ve done really,
really well there and exceeded the target

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:03.210
we set ourselves.

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:11.700
Of particular interest, I think, is that 400,000
of those 600,000 people by 2021 will receive

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:17.790
talking therapies as part of their treatment
for their long-term physical health conditions.

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:23.559
What we know is that you are many times more
likely to suffer from depression and anxiety

00:41:23.559 --> 00:41:30.099
if you have a diagnosis of a long-term condition
such as diabetes or respiratory problems,

00:41:30.099 --> 00:41:34.280
or cardiac problems: something like four or
five times more likely to be depressed.

00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:35.280
Guess what?

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:38.720
When you’re depressed, you don’t exercise
well, you don’t eat well, you don’t socialise

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:44.590
as well, and the impact then on your physical
health, and so a downward spiral.

00:41:44.590 --> 00:41:53.089
This past year, we’ve seen 37 pilot funded
programmes for talking therapies in long-term

00:41:53.089 --> 00:41:57.180
condition management, and we are – we’re
going to do a formal evaluation; we’re just

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:00.680
in the process of collection all of the outcomes.

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:06.140
But we know from the informal evaluations
that have been done that that programme has

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:13.710
had a stunning impact on people’s physical
health, their attendances, therefore, in primary

00:42:13.710 --> 00:42:20.180
care and seeing their GP, and at A&amp;E and,
indeed, admission all the way through to medical

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:21.180
wards.

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:25.720
We’ve seen in places like Cambridge and
Peterborough in excess of a 70% reduction

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:30.339
in GP attendances for the cohorts of patients
receiving this therapy.

00:42:30.339 --> 00:42:34.359
And again, across the country, I think we’ll
see really big dips.

00:42:34.359 --> 00:42:40.660
We’ve set up eating disorder services in
the past 18 months across the country for

00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:42.040
children and young people.

00:42:42.040 --> 00:42:44.730
This was an area of high need identified.

00:42:44.730 --> 00:42:50.890
And again, I think there were very few of
those services when we began our programme,

00:42:50.890 --> 00:42:55.609
and now every part of the country, children
and young people have access to those services,

00:42:55.609 --> 00:43:03.450
and not only access, but we have a very rigorous
referral to treatment standard, so one week

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:07.200
for urgent referrals and four weeks for all
others.

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:12.450
And certainly as I go about the country and
talk to families who have had access to this

00:43:12.450 --> 00:43:17.660
service, and ones who didn’t whose youngster
developed problems in the absence of such

00:43:17.660 --> 00:43:24.130
services, we are seeing really different pathways
into care, much more rapid referral from schools,

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:29.200
primary care, earlier intervention with the
youngster, their family, and shorter, therefore,

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:30.599
episodes of treatment.

00:43:30.599 --> 00:43:36.930
Again, it’s too early to see a big academic
formal evaluation of those outcomes, but I’m

00:43:36.930 --> 00:43:40.560
confident when it comes, we will see real
improvements there.

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:45.119
And I think just the last highlight – because
you can read about the others here – it’s

00:43:45.119 --> 00:43:52.980
been our drive here to devolve through a sort
of new care models programme – and this

00:43:52.980 --> 00:43:57.859
Board did consider a paper on this previously,
so I won’t go into a lot of detail here

00:43:57.859 --> 00:44:03.570
– but to devolve the specialist commissioning
budget through to provider-led collaborations

00:44:03.570 --> 00:44:08.870
locally, as part of STPs or integrated care
systems.

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:14.619
And we’ve managed to devolve now, to date,
approximately £700 million of the £1.8 billion

00:44:14.619 --> 00:44:19.790
budget, and we’re now looking to the next
year or two to do that further.

00:44:19.790 --> 00:44:20.790
Why?

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:25.759
Because it brings that money, the responsibility
and the patient population, the needs of those

00:44:25.759 --> 00:44:29.619
patient populations, much closer to local
control.

00:44:29.619 --> 00:44:33.980
It’s lending itself to redesign of services
in the community.

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:38.970
Just to be clear, these are usually highly
expensive beds for either people with forensic

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:44.790
challenges, or children and young people with
real challenges.

00:44:44.790 --> 00:44:50.160
It brings the responsibility for those patients
closer to home, and what we’re seeing already

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:58.140
is a reduction in out-of-area distant treatment,
but also an increase in community developments

00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:01.060
in the intensive care community space.

00:45:01.060 --> 00:45:02.840
This is a very exciting programme.

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:06.940
So, you can read about our other achievements;
they’re multiple and many.

00:45:06.940 --> 00:45:10.640
I’d like to leave time for questions.

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:17.030
I think we have to be clear that this is a
challenging programme, and some of the challenges

00:45:17.030 --> 00:45:20.660
that we particularly encounter are around
workforce.

00:45:20.660 --> 00:45:25.930
We released the Stepping – or Health Education
England released the Stepping Forward report

00:45:25.930 --> 00:45:32.849
last summer, which really talks to 19,000
additional staff required, approximately,

00:45:32.849 --> 00:45:39.579
to deliver our programme, to approximately
1 million extra people by 2021, so this isn’t

00:45:39.579 --> 00:45:41.290
a programme that powers itself.

00:45:41.290 --> 00:45:44.160
So, I think that’s a challenge.

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:50.460
We have funded several, now, global digital
exemplars, and some fast followers, which

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:51.770
is terrific.

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:59.740
But I think we are in a hurry to see the roll-out
of more digital application, greater interoperability,

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:04.230
across the acute primary care mental health
space, but we’ve made some good inroads

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:05.230
there.

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:12.750
And I think I would come back to the challenges
of making sure that wider society are also

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:18.810
doing their bit, whether it’s for children
and young people or adults, because we are

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:23.940
seeing an increase in demand and need for
mental health services in line with other

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:30.859
NHS services, and we believe that some of
this is driven by what goes on, if you like,

00:46:30.859 --> 00:46:34.540
in tier 1, tier 2 and preventative areas.

00:46:34.540 --> 00:46:39.020
We’re not only about – I’m nearly going
to stop, because I’d like questions, but

00:46:39.020 --> 00:46:43.750
there are some other very big areas of work
that we’re also involved in beyond and around

00:46:43.750 --> 00:46:44.990
the Five Year Forward View.

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:51.849
So, on the quality front, we’ve been working
with NHSI on improving quality, and we’ve

00:46:51.849 --> 00:47:00.030
seen the provider trust sector move from RI
to good overwhelmingly in this past year to

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:02.859
18 months, which is obviously really encouraging.

00:47:02.859 --> 00:47:09.650
We’re working with Simon Wesley on the new
Mental Health Act review; that’s something

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:14.849
he’s leading, but clearly, we want to make
sure that any recommendations or changes from

00:47:14.849 --> 00:47:17.230
that can have practical application.

00:47:17.230 --> 00:47:21.760
And we’re also interested particularly in
the new Mental Health Act from an equalities

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:29.530
point of view; we know that young black African-Caribbean
mean are overdetained, what leads to that.

00:47:29.530 --> 00:47:34.890
It’s often what happens before the point
of detention, not the point of detention,

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:37.079
where things have gone wrong.

00:47:37.079 --> 00:47:41.730
And of course, the Government’s green paper
for children and young people, where they

00:47:41.730 --> 00:47:48.150
are considering the outcome of consultation
on this big new programme to provide more

00:47:48.150 --> 00:47:54.160
counselling services in schools, and we as
NHS England and across the country need to

00:47:54.160 --> 00:47:58.849
be very involved in making sure that that
lands well for local people.

00:47:58.849 --> 00:48:01.470
So, we’re not without our challenges.

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:06.700
You can see where we’ve made huge inroads,
and I will just conclude on the money.

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:11.960
There’s a lot of focus and attention – money
and workforce are probably the two big challenges.

00:48:11.960 --> 00:48:19.710
I think we have done really well to adhere
to our commitment to honour the mental health

00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:26.589
investment standard, which in shorthand, basically
means we will spend at least as much, and

00:48:26.589 --> 00:48:32.000
ideally more, on the uplift of mental health
services as we do on the uplift – you know,

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:34.680
the entire NHS programme uplift.

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:39.869
And we’ve managed to achieve that in the
first two years of the programme.

00:48:39.869 --> 00:48:46.290
In this third year, we’ve tightened our
– or we’ve increased our expectations

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:51.440
further still, and in particular, what we
have done – and this was I think a tough

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:56.900
and courageous decision, if I might say – is,
we’ve applied a no-exceptions rule.

00:48:56.900 --> 00:49:04.470
So, even to those health economies that are
most distressed, we’ve said that mental

00:49:04.470 --> 00:49:09.430
health – actually, the mental health uplift,
the standard, must apply.

00:49:09.430 --> 00:49:14.400
In the ‘18/19 plans, which we’re still
assuring, so I can’t give the definitive

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:21.810
outcome here, but it’s looking really encouraging
that all areas will meet that standard, so

00:49:21.810 --> 00:49:26.480
making sure that the money flows to the frontline
is an incredibly important part of this programme,

00:49:26.480 --> 00:49:27.480
as well.

00:49:27.480 --> 00:49:29.990
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Claire, thank you very much.

00:49:29.990 --> 00:49:30.990
Great presentation.

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:33.910
Can I now invite questions?

00:49:33.910 --> 00:49:35.540
Noel, Victor and Michelle.

00:49:35.540 --> 00:49:40.660
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Claire, thank you very much
for your very skilled leadership in this.

00:49:40.660 --> 00:49:47.369
You’ll know – or maybe remember from conversations
that you and I have that I’m particularly

00:49:47.369 --> 00:49:50.420
focused on transition from school to university.

00:49:50.420 --> 00:49:51.420
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yes.

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:56.099
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: And I think a lot of parents
will be interested in what steps we need to

00:49:56.099 --> 00:50:05.430
take in conjunction with the Department for
Education to address the increase in mental

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:12.130
health issues at university, and, you know,
it’s manifesting itself in so many different

00:50:12.130 --> 00:50:17.740
ways, from eating disorders, right the way
through to the increase in suicides that we’ve

00:50:17.740 --> 00:50:19.390
had recently.

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:22.470
What steps do you think we need to take?

00:50:22.470 --> 00:50:30.180
Apart from the green paper on child prevention
and treatment in schools, what steps do you

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:35.990
think we need to take in universities to get
a better mental health service at the point

00:50:35.990 --> 00:50:36.990
of need?

00:50:36.990 --> 00:50:42.230
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yes, well, we do know – there
was an excellent report released recently

00:50:42.230 --> 00:50:47.880
looking at student mental health, and something
I had not appreciated was now half of our

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:49.049
young go to university.

00:50:49.049 --> 00:50:55.730
I hadn’t realised that the figure was as
high as 50% of our young are now in university.

00:50:55.730 --> 00:51:02.559
We’ve seen over the last three years a reduction
in suicide, just to talk to your suicide question.

00:51:02.559 --> 00:51:08.720
We’ve seen a reduction in suicides nationally,
which is a good thing, but within that, there

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:13.599
are segments of the population where we’ve
seen an increase, and indeed, that sort of

00:51:13.599 --> 00:51:19.290
age range of about 20 years old is an area
where we’ve seen an increase.

00:51:19.290 --> 00:51:24.430
We haven’t yet consolidated – in response
to the report that came out two weeks ago

00:51:24.430 --> 00:51:27.050
– what our plan is.

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:30.760
We did have a meeting with the Permanent Secretary
for Education recently.

00:51:30.760 --> 00:51:33.490
Simon and I went along there.

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:37.829
But it’s quite clear that there are some
things that we can do relatively quickly,

00:51:37.829 --> 00:51:42.799
which the team here are looking at now, in
particular, in light of the report a couple

00:51:42.799 --> 00:51:43.799
of weeks ago.

00:51:43.799 --> 00:51:50.550
We’re looking at this issue of primary care
and how we make sure that the GPs across the

00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:57.660
home town and the student town are aligned,
particularly with youngsters that go to university

00:51:57.660 --> 00:51:59.070
with pre-existing issues.

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:03.550
So, we’re just doing a bit of work – don’t
know the outcome of it yet – which Ian Dodge

00:52:03.550 --> 00:52:05.310
is helping with.

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:10.609
Indeed, we were – I think we were emailing
at midnight one night last week, but – about

00:52:10.609 --> 00:52:11.690
how we take it forward.

00:52:11.690 --> 00:52:16.880
So, we’re looking at that greater continuity
of primary care across, for those who go with

00:52:16.880 --> 00:52:18.220
pre-existing problems.

00:52:18.220 --> 00:52:25.369
And I’ve asked my team to look at, are we
sure that access into the IAPT, the talking

00:52:25.369 --> 00:52:27.950
therapies programme, is seamless and easy?

00:52:27.950 --> 00:52:33.460
So, people can refer themselves into IAPT
services, we encourage it.

00:52:33.460 --> 00:52:39.619
I should have said that last year, we saw
more than – well, about a million people

00:52:39.619 --> 00:52:45.390
coming into talking therapies last year, with
better recovery rates, and so on and so forth,

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:46.540
than we’ve ever had.

00:52:46.540 --> 00:52:52.539
So, we’re really keen that, this year, we’re
expanding access into those talking therapies

00:52:52.539 --> 00:52:53.539
services.

00:52:53.539 --> 00:52:57.970
They exist everywhere across the country,
so I’ve asked my team here to look at what

00:52:57.970 --> 00:53:03.520
work we can do with the universities on really
encouraging either self-referral or rapid

00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:07.700
access into our talking therapies programme.

00:53:07.700 --> 00:53:15.430
Those talking therapies programmes are well
versed and highly skilled in knowing when

00:53:15.430 --> 00:53:19.990
people they see need more than basic psychological
counselling.

00:53:19.990 --> 00:53:25.960
So, they will also help signpost youngsters.

00:53:25.960 --> 00:53:31.190
The last thing we’re doing is obviously
liaising very closely with those university

00:53:31.190 --> 00:53:37.829
towns and those universities that have had
specific issues and problems.

00:53:37.829 --> 00:53:39.770
We saw Bristol on the news.

00:53:39.770 --> 00:53:44.000
So, we’re looking at, what is Public Health
doing, what’s our role there, how do we

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:45.700
support a real focus?

00:53:45.700 --> 00:53:52.610
Why do some universities do better than others
in terms of pastoral care?

00:53:52.610 --> 00:54:00.359
I think in the longer term, we will be looking
at the whole pathway for young people, perhaps

00:54:00.359 --> 00:54:06.700
from nought to perhaps beyond 18, and that’s
something that I suspect we’ll be working

00:54:06.700 --> 00:54:07.700
on with folk going forward.

00:54:07.700 --> 00:54:10.609
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Claire, I’ve got quite
a few Board members anxious to –

00:54:10.609 --> 00:54:11.819
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Keep the answers short.

00:54:11.819 --> 00:54:13.039
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yeah, that’d be helpful.

00:54:13.039 --> 00:54:17.010
I’ve got in a holding pattern at the moment
Victor, Michelle, Wendy and David, and there

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:20.050
may well be others.

00:54:20.050 --> 00:54:21.950
But Victor, please, over to you.

00:54:21.950 --> 00:54:23.760
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Thank you, Claire.

00:54:23.760 --> 00:54:24.840
Really good report.

00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:28.100
Strong and enthusiastic leadership, which
is clearly showing results.

00:54:28.100 --> 00:54:29.180
Three quick questions.

00:54:29.180 --> 00:54:34.400
Firstly, all the cohorts that you mention
in general in the report include BME people,

00:54:34.400 --> 00:54:41.700
and last time I looked, all of them experienced
worse experience and worse outcomes in those

00:54:41.700 --> 00:54:42.700
cohorts.

00:54:42.700 --> 00:54:48.559
And you particularly mention IAPT and the
experience and outcomes for BME populations;

00:54:48.559 --> 00:54:53.220
it frankly is disturbing, just on IAPT.

00:54:53.220 --> 00:55:00.950
I was just wondering, how are we capturing
the measurements, the – of both experience

00:55:00.950 --> 00:55:07.260
and outcomes across those – across BME people,
not just young black men, which we’ve known

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:14.730
about for a long time, but young black women,
children, that appear to have the – are

00:55:14.730 --> 00:55:16.619
challenged by mental health?

00:55:16.619 --> 00:55:22.270
And the third question – well, I suppose
the second question – is, you talked about

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:26.359
detention, and I think you’re absolutely
right; the increase of – the disproportionate

00:55:26.359 --> 00:55:30.309
use of detention and the impact on BME communities
is true.

00:55:30.309 --> 00:55:34.099
But I think there is also the question of
what happens in relation to the police.

00:55:34.099 --> 00:55:38.549
Me and you were both on that commission, which
did, I think, produce some – well, I would

00:55:38.549 --> 00:55:40.700
say, wouldn’t I, I chaired it – some good
work.

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:43.680
Well, it moved the needle.

00:55:43.680 --> 00:55:49.650
What are relationships – you know, what
are we doing in relation to the police to

00:55:49.650 --> 00:55:56.680
ensure that once the threshold’s crossed,
we’re getting – you know, we’re moving

00:55:56.680 --> 00:56:00.559
the needle or we continue to move the needle
in the direction of safety and proper support?

00:56:00.559 --> 00:56:07.230
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: So, just in terms of your
first question, when this programme began

00:56:07.230 --> 00:56:14.089
two years ago, we – our data collection
was difficult.

00:56:14.089 --> 00:56:20.440
I think it was fair to say that we collected
lots of data but not always the right data,

00:56:20.440 --> 00:56:22.470
and not always reliably.

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:26.310
And I think that when – before you can start
measuring outcomes, you’d better make sure

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:32.180
that, for example, now the mental health minimum
data set is something that we’re using,

00:56:32.180 --> 00:56:35.710
that we’re adhering to much more, we’re
transferring.

00:56:35.710 --> 00:56:40.069
Where we have one-off collections of information
because they didn’t exist before the start

00:56:40.069 --> 00:56:45.860
of this programme, we’re now moving from
one-off collections into much more regularised

00:56:45.860 --> 00:56:49.089
systematic collecting of data.

00:56:49.089 --> 00:56:54.461
The mental health minimum data set will absolutely
lend itself, when it’s been regularly and

00:56:54.461 --> 00:57:02.200
reliably populated, and we’ve made huge
progress, to far greater analysis of individuals

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:07.450
and their make-up, so not just ethnicity,
but we’re hoping to do work matching that,

00:57:07.450 --> 00:57:13.920
as you see in the report, also to census and
locality information, so that will be gender,

00:57:13.920 --> 00:57:14.920
as well.

00:57:14.920 --> 00:57:18.299
So, that’s a big piece of work that’s
in train at the moment.

00:57:18.299 --> 00:57:24.010
And we have just recently actually recruited
the very splendid Jacqui Dyer to help give

00:57:24.010 --> 00:57:27.789
us advice on the particular issue of race
and equalities, on the work we’re doing

00:57:27.789 --> 00:57:29.740
and what we’re collecting.

00:57:29.740 --> 00:57:34.309
I don’t think we collect enough on outcomes
across the board, at the moment.

00:57:34.309 --> 00:57:40.950
I think our first two years have been about
establishing programme management, publishing

00:57:40.950 --> 00:57:48.010
in the dashboards the data we do collect,
establishing the regional and local delivery

00:57:48.010 --> 00:57:52.160
mechanism, and setting up some of these new
services that I spoke to you about earlier,

00:57:52.160 --> 00:57:53.289
so widening access.

00:57:53.289 --> 00:57:58.109
And all of those have been our single biggest
priority in a busy two years.

00:57:58.109 --> 00:58:04.789
But it’s absolutely time now – and we’ve
begun that, you can see from the paper – to

00:58:04.789 --> 00:58:13.690
move to far more insight-driven collection
of information that helps us understand the

00:58:13.690 --> 00:58:19.010
impacts of deprivation, particularly the BME
agenda, but also socioeconomic deprivation.

00:58:19.010 --> 00:58:21.779
I mentioned suicide earlier.

00:58:21.779 --> 00:58:28.529
We are seeing some great reduction in suicide
at the moment, considerable material reductions,

00:58:28.529 --> 00:58:33.170
but certain parts of the country we’re not,
and the suicide rate is much higher.

00:58:33.170 --> 00:58:37.809
So, we’re hoping to get much better at understanding
that in the next three years.

00:58:37.809 --> 00:58:44.589
I appreciate that, for you and perhaps for
me and my colleagues, we’re not as far forward

00:58:44.589 --> 00:58:48.420
with that as we’d like to be, but we’ve
simply had to prioritise these other things,

00:58:48.420 --> 00:58:53.380
to date, but we’re moving to a different
space now.

00:58:53.380 --> 00:59:00.790
And I think – it’s my hope that, as we
– as the dashboard matures over the next

00:59:00.790 --> 00:59:08.100
three to five years, much more of this information
will be there and available for challenge

00:59:08.100 --> 00:59:09.330
and scrutiny.

00:59:09.330 --> 00:59:14.170
On the police front, I think we’ve made
some big headway this year.

00:59:14.170 --> 00:59:22.790
The numbers of people detained on a 136 in
a police cell have just reduced dramatically,

00:59:22.790 --> 00:59:28.559
and actually, since the commission we both
sat on in London a few years ago, we’ve

00:59:28.559 --> 00:59:34.490
reduced – London has a very high number
of Section 136s, more than 4,000 a year, and

00:59:34.490 --> 00:59:38.640
at that time, quite a few people were detained
in police stations on a 136.

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:43.020
Now, it’s – I haven’t looked at the
latest figures, but it’ll be something like

00:59:43.020 --> 00:59:45.109
ten or 11 in a year are.

00:59:45.109 --> 00:59:50.299
So, we’re getting much – what that signifies
is that the NHS and the police – and we’re

00:59:50.299 --> 00:59:54.299
seeing other similarly impressive results
across the country – are getting much better

00:59:54.299 --> 00:59:58.780
at working together around smoother and more
timely handover.

00:59:58.780 --> 01:00:05.820
Also, the Met Police – but this is true
of forces across the country – are doing

01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:11.010
much more training now in mental health than
they ever did before, and joint work in mental

01:00:11.010 --> 01:00:12.010
health.

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:13.579
So, I think there’s progress in that space.

01:00:13.579 --> 01:00:17.490
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Victor, you may want to come
back on that, but before you do, can I get

01:00:17.490 --> 01:00:19.859
some of the other questions into the mix?

01:00:19.859 --> 01:00:20.859
So, Michelle?

01:00:20.859 --> 01:00:23.299
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Thank you, Chair.

01:00:23.299 --> 01:00:29.140
Just briefly, what’s your overall confidence
rate in achieving the main objectives by the

01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:31.010
end of the programme?

01:00:31.010 --> 01:00:37.500
And could you talk about workforce as a risk
to the programme at the moment?

01:00:37.500 --> 01:00:41.770
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: My overall confidence rate
is good, it’s strong.

01:00:41.770 --> 01:00:46.500
It’s not to say the programme is without
risks or it won’t be challenging, but you

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:53.000
can see from the first two years, the level
of engagement, the effectiveness of the systems

01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:59.859
and processes we’ve set up, and the appetite,
actually the appetite out there to make this

01:00:59.859 --> 01:01:05.630
happen, is in my opinion – you know, are
all reasons, are all indicators to be confident

01:01:05.630 --> 01:01:07.400
but not complacent.

01:01:07.400 --> 01:01:14.520
This will be a challenge, and workforce is
likely the biggest of those challenges.

01:01:14.520 --> 01:01:18.569
So, we’re working very closely with Health
Education England.

01:01:18.569 --> 01:01:25.150
We asked – we think it’s an indicative
19,000 more to power these changes.

01:01:25.150 --> 01:01:31.110
If areas are further advanced on digital application,
for example, they might find they need fewer

01:01:31.110 --> 01:01:33.890
workforce, but it’s for them to decide.

01:01:33.890 --> 01:01:39.190
So, we’ve just called in the regional plans,
we’re checking all of those at STP level

01:01:39.190 --> 01:01:43.059
now, and we’re about to set – begin measuring
the trajectory.

01:01:43.059 --> 01:01:44.520
So, it will be tough.

01:01:44.520 --> 01:01:49.990
Areas are looking at the creation of new roles,
different ways of funding people into nurse

01:01:49.990 --> 01:01:50.990
training.

01:01:50.990 --> 01:01:56.010
People are making greater use of their apprenticeship
levies, which I think it’s true to say we

01:01:56.010 --> 01:02:01.569
didn’t make good use of in year one; I think
people didn’t realise how you could use

01:02:01.569 --> 01:02:02.569
it.

01:02:02.569 --> 01:02:09.140
So, I think we’re doing some good work with
NHSI as well on retention.

01:02:09.140 --> 01:02:14.839
We know that the opportunity for retention
in mental health is greater, so people leave

01:02:14.839 --> 01:02:19.299
earlier from mental health than they do from
the general sector.

01:02:19.299 --> 01:02:20.450
Why is that?

01:02:20.450 --> 01:02:25.960
Could employers in the mental health space
– in the mental health sector do more to

01:02:25.960 --> 01:02:27.050
retain staff?

01:02:27.050 --> 01:02:33.030
So, NHSI have been running a really very good
programme of – with providers, they’ve

01:02:33.030 --> 01:02:38.400
done three cohorts so far, really looking
at the steps we’re taking to keep people

01:02:38.400 --> 01:02:40.390
in mental health employment.

01:02:40.390 --> 01:02:48.260
So, it’s not just the recruiting of the
new; it’s the keeping of the current –

01:02:48.260 --> 01:02:51.300
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: It’s very exciting, but
I’ve got more questions waiting to come

01:02:51.300 --> 01:02:52.300
in.

01:02:52.300 --> 01:02:54.180
So, can I please bring in Wendy?

01:02:54.180 --> 01:02:56.690
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Yes, thank you for that.

01:02:56.690 --> 01:02:57.950
Really terrific progress on a number of things.

01:02:57.950 --> 01:03:02.990
I’m very concerned and focused on young
people’s mental health, because it makes

01:03:02.990 --> 01:03:06.640
such a difference if we’re going to factor
issues among young people in terms of overall

01:03:06.640 --> 01:03:09.269
life satisfaction.

01:03:09.269 --> 01:03:14.049
One of the things that I’m most concerned
about is that transition in a patient-centred

01:03:14.049 --> 01:03:16.180
way from being a young person to being older.

01:03:16.180 --> 01:03:22.270
A bit of variation, I guess, on Noel’s question,
but how is a system beyond just the kids who

01:03:22.270 --> 01:03:26.279
are going to university, how are we making
sure we’re working with patients through

01:03:26.279 --> 01:03:28.279
that transition and through that journey?

01:03:28.279 --> 01:03:30.440
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yeah.

01:03:30.440 --> 01:03:33.720
I think the transition issue is critical.

01:03:33.720 --> 01:03:39.990
A, do we need to transition at 18, is a bigger
question than I can answer, but I think it’s

01:03:39.990 --> 01:03:41.700
a question we need to address.

01:03:41.700 --> 01:03:47.200
I think that we are going to have – we’re
working with other agencies on this at the

01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:53.079
moment – quite a focus on, what’s the
quality of transition now?

01:03:53.079 --> 01:03:58.119
Is there a set way of doing it that makes
sure you always make the kinds of plans that

01:03:58.119 --> 01:03:59.119
you need to?

01:03:59.119 --> 01:04:05.140
Are you always talking to the young people
themselves about that transition and engaging

01:04:05.140 --> 01:04:06.190
them in it?

01:04:06.190 --> 01:04:10.410
And some parts of the country do it really
well, we know, because we’ve been and looked,

01:04:10.410 --> 01:04:16.260
and other parts of the country could do it
much better, so we will be working with them

01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:23.450
and with NHSI over the next three years to
improve the quality of that transition.

01:04:23.450 --> 01:04:27.970
It’s a critical point in people’s – young
people’s lives.

01:04:27.970 --> 01:04:32.070
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Malcolm, can I just highlight
that the Commissioning Committee, we do spend

01:04:32.070 --> 01:04:36.530
– three times a year, we have a very in-depth
conversation on mental health, and going through

01:04:36.530 --> 01:04:37.980
that as we do all the national programmes.

01:04:37.980 --> 01:04:39.660
So, we look forward to continuing that conversation.

01:04:39.660 --> 01:04:40.660
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yeah.

01:04:40.660 --> 01:04:41.660
So, that will be great.

01:04:41.660 --> 01:04:45.450
I would just say, last week was Mental Health
Awareness Week, and we had a whole week backed

01:04:45.450 --> 01:04:51.079
by the brilliant comms team and others here,
and across the regions, and we kicked that

01:04:51.079 --> 01:04:53.740
week off – we had a whole series of events.

01:04:53.740 --> 01:04:59.109
We kicked the week off last Saturday with
a young person’s mental health conference

01:04:59.109 --> 01:05:00.960
in London.

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:05.450
We did that with – together with Young Minds,
who are really helping us – these youth

01:05:05.450 --> 01:05:11.580
activists are working with us to change policy,
change practice and help us think about the

01:05:11.580 --> 01:05:12.580
future.

01:05:12.580 --> 01:05:17.720
It was an incredible conference, and the thing
that struck me was the extent to which these

01:05:17.720 --> 01:05:21.810
young people know what needs to happen by
way of change in design.

01:05:21.810 --> 01:05:28.920
They very much wanted to talk about this transition
issue; they had heaps of practical, good suggestions

01:05:28.920 --> 01:05:33.809
about what would help them, and we’re actively
engaging with Young Minds on that issue, as

01:05:33.809 --> 01:05:34.809
well.

01:05:34.809 --> 01:05:37.119
But boy, are they taking the mental health
debate to society.

01:05:37.119 --> 01:05:38.839
They are challenging stigma; they’re going
to demand more of us all.

01:05:38.839 --> 01:05:39.839
Formidable.

01:05:39.839 --> 01:05:41.079
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: David?

01:05:41.079 --> 01:05:48.940
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, Claire, I’ve had the
benefit of following your journey over the

01:05:48.940 --> 01:05:53.150
two years since – through the Commissioning
Committee with the regular interactions, as

01:05:53.150 --> 01:05:54.150
Wendy said.

01:05:54.150 --> 01:05:57.250
And actually, it’s less of a question; it’s
more of an observation.

01:05:57.250 --> 01:06:00.510
I think you and the team have done a fabulous
job.

01:06:00.510 --> 01:06:03.849
You know, you’ve taken a subject that was
– I’ll give this man a little bit of credit,

01:06:03.849 --> 01:06:04.940
because he actually sort of –
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: He does deserve some, you

01:06:04.940 --> 01:06:05.940
know –
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: But not too much, not too

01:06:05.940 --> 01:06:08.369
much, because he gets too much elsewhere –
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: No, obviously, but he does

01:06:08.369 --> 01:06:09.369
deserve some.

01:06:09.369 --> 01:06:13.769
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: But actually, I think you
and the team have done a super job about taking

01:06:13.769 --> 01:06:19.339
an issue that was in the margins and making
it into the mainstream.

01:06:19.339 --> 01:06:25.390
And also raising people’s awareness and
expectation that mental health is as important

01:06:25.390 --> 01:06:29.099
as physical health, in lots of cases, more
important.

01:06:29.099 --> 01:06:33.500
And I just think we should just pay tribute
to you and your team for a great job done.

01:06:33.500 --> 01:06:39.279
My observation would be, we’ve talked a
lot about children, rightly, a lot about young

01:06:39.279 --> 01:06:42.190
adults, rightly.

01:06:42.190 --> 01:06:47.170
When you come back next time, I’d be intrigued,
given the demographic changes that are going

01:06:47.170 --> 01:06:51.960
on, to understand a bit more about how we’re
focusing on older groups.

01:06:51.960 --> 01:06:57.920
Because I observed, I think, you know, the
sense of isolation, the sense of loneliness,

01:06:57.920 --> 01:07:02.460
the sense of – particularly, you made the
point about people with long-term conditions,

01:07:02.460 --> 01:07:05.019
how that impacts them.

01:07:05.019 --> 01:07:10.839
And I completely understand the prioritisation,
and it’s right, but we mustn’t also forget

01:07:10.839 --> 01:07:15.980
that group of people who are going to grow
in number over the coming years.

01:07:15.980 --> 01:07:21.570
So, not for now, but next time, maybe just
a focus on that as well as on the young.

01:07:21.570 --> 01:07:22.570
But well done.

01:07:22.570 --> 01:07:23.849
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Two things very quickly.

01:07:23.849 --> 01:07:25.049
One, thank you.

01:07:25.049 --> 01:07:29.079
And I – it would be quite wrong of me to
be here today and not mention Karen Turner

01:07:29.079 --> 01:07:30.550
–
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Yes, indeed, absolutely.

01:07:30.550 --> 01:07:34.920
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: – who leaves the mental
health programme this week, and who was a

01:07:34.920 --> 01:07:40.030
forerunner to me, and did an awful lot of
the heavy lifting together with other colleagues,

01:07:40.030 --> 01:07:47.019
with Treasury, with Simon’s backing, and
really began the – you know, the beginnings

01:07:47.019 --> 01:07:48.430
of setting up this programme.

01:07:48.430 --> 01:07:52.760
I think she’s been terrific, and I would
like to publicly thank Karen and wish her

01:07:52.760 --> 01:07:53.960
well on her next step of the way.

01:07:53.960 --> 01:07:58.710
In terms of me, crikey, what a massive privilege
this is.

01:07:58.710 --> 01:08:03.119
And Simon was true to his word when I said
to him, ‘If I do this, I need to know that

01:08:03.119 --> 01:08:05.520
everybody really means it, because I do’.

01:08:05.520 --> 01:08:11.650
And I have to say that this Board and colleagues
across NHS England are really interested in

01:08:11.650 --> 01:08:12.650
this.

01:08:12.650 --> 01:08:18.029
And it is the Trojan horse, I believe, more
psychologically and emotionally minded care,

01:08:18.029 --> 01:08:25.190
the IAPT programme, the Liaison Service, these
are the programmes that will start to transform

01:08:25.190 --> 01:08:30.219
healthcare in its broadest sense, as well
as better services for people with serious

01:08:30.219 --> 01:08:31.219
mental illness.

01:08:31.219 --> 01:08:32.380
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Very good.

01:08:32.380 --> 01:08:34.170
Well, just a final comment.

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:39.609
Again, we’re going to be later on talking
about the combining of the senior leadership

01:08:39.609 --> 01:08:44.920
for NHS England and NHS Improvement, and I
think we can look forward to Claire’s role

01:08:44.920 --> 01:08:50.380
expanding to be able to have an energising
impact across both organisations, which is

01:08:50.380 --> 01:08:55.940
going to allow us to make further progress
over the coming years.

01:08:55.940 --> 01:09:01.699
But one thing we haven’t talked about, just
to record, is that it’s also Claire’s

01:09:01.699 --> 01:09:06.159
staff who are providing the mental health
support for the people who were affected by

01:09:06.159 --> 01:09:13.449
Grenfell Tower, and that’s true today as
the enquiry gets underway, as we’ve seen

01:09:13.449 --> 01:09:16.409
traumatic occasions, even in the last two
days.

01:09:16.409 --> 01:09:21.250
So, I think a big thank you to Claire’s
staff particularly who are supporting everybody

01:09:21.250 --> 01:09:22.500
associated with Grenfell.

01:09:22.500 --> 01:09:25.100
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you, Simon.

01:09:25.100 --> 01:09:32.310
And might I say, I think Grenfell will teach
us all many lessons, because the community

01:09:32.310 --> 01:09:37.460
there and some of the third-sector organisations
that we’re working with – in fact, Turning

01:09:37.460 --> 01:09:45.239
Point, Hestia, others – the community themselves
are taking this opportunity to take some control

01:09:45.239 --> 01:09:50.389
over their healthcare and think about what
they want the future to look like in terms

01:09:50.389 --> 01:09:54.130
of their own health and wellbeing, and so
it’s a privilege.

01:09:54.130 --> 01:09:59.980
And you’re right, yes, staff are in the
enquiry right now with people, they’ve been

01:09:59.980 --> 01:10:04.400
working with the police, the fire services,
a tremendous effort.

01:10:04.400 --> 01:10:07.730
But again, a rather sad privilege.

01:10:07.730 --> 01:10:15.360
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: One of the advantages of
being the chairman is you get the last word.

01:10:15.360 --> 01:10:16.360
Well, usually.

01:10:16.360 --> 01:10:21.700
But this one, I think things that you need
to know are, when the Board sets out on an

01:10:21.700 --> 01:10:26.850
enterprise such as this, we do so from quite
a high level of extraction.

01:10:26.850 --> 01:10:31.610
We drill down as far as we can to try and
discover what we need to do, what the priorities

01:10:31.610 --> 01:10:36.090
are, and we get reports back, but it’s only
when we hear from the people who are actually

01:10:36.090 --> 01:10:40.630
leading the frontline that we get a real sense
of what it’s like, as opposed to what the

01:10:40.630 --> 01:10:42.100
data tell us, and you’ve given us both today.

01:10:42.100 --> 01:10:48.489
I think the data are really very impressive,
by the way, and I think the Board would like

01:10:48.489 --> 01:10:53.040
to express yet again its confidence in the
programme and its confidence in you in leading

01:10:53.040 --> 01:10:58.340
it, and our shared commitment to making sure
that this is carried the whole way through.

01:10:58.340 --> 01:11:03.690
These are not just short-term objectives,
but medium- and long-term objectives.

01:11:03.690 --> 01:11:08.659
David’s given you an indication of another
area that we might want to come and revisit

01:11:08.659 --> 01:11:13.660
on a future visit, but on behalf of the whole
Board, can I just say many thanks, and we

01:11:13.660 --> 01:11:21.670
look forward next time also, in how we through
our work with NHSI are able to overcome some

01:11:21.670 --> 01:11:25.219
of the obstacles and some of the challenges
that we’ve talked about today in a more

01:11:25.219 --> 01:11:26.820
joined-up national leadership.

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:29.130
But thank you again for coming along today.

01:11:29.130 --> 01:11:34.650
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you, and I have a
terrific team.

01:11:34.650 --> 01:11:36.230
The last word.

01:11:36.230 --> 01:11:38.420
But thank you.

01:11:38.420 --> 01:11:40.800
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: The last word.

01:11:40.800 --> 01:11:45.170
Okay, I knew I wasn’t going to win.

01:11:45.170 --> 01:11:46.739
Okay, right.

01:11:46.739 --> 01:11:47.739
Right.

01:11:47.739 --> 01:11:51.920
The next item is Academic Health Science Networks,
and it’s Ian.

01:11:51.920 --> 01:11:54.290
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, Chair.

01:11:54.290 --> 01:11:58.440
So, why today are we discussing Academic Health
Science Networks?

01:11:58.440 --> 01:12:02.449
The NHS is a complex, adaptive system.

01:12:02.449 --> 01:12:07.740
Arguably, it’s needlessly complex and insufficiently
adaptive.

01:12:07.740 --> 01:12:14.429
And to flourish in its next 70 years, it will
need to become ever smarter at developing

01:12:14.429 --> 01:12:21.239
and incubating proven innovations, and then
critically, spreading these faster.

01:12:21.239 --> 01:12:27.560
We have world-beating traditions in basic
research and breakthrough medicines.

01:12:27.560 --> 01:12:32.370
We have Biobank, NIHR, UKRI.

01:12:32.370 --> 01:12:35.100
Our pioneering work, Malcolm, with Genomics
England.

01:12:35.100 --> 01:12:38.330
A thriving life sciences sector.

01:12:38.330 --> 01:12:43.090
And potentially the best longitudinal data
sets in the world.

01:12:43.090 --> 01:12:50.620
And between the NHS, industry, government,
academia, more can be done to make the most

01:12:50.620 --> 01:12:58.239
of our extraordinary assets, both to generate
value for the NHS, and, wherever possible,

01:12:58.239 --> 01:13:06.590
also benefit the UK economy by generating
jobs, inward investment and export wins, helping

01:13:06.590 --> 01:13:12.030
startups and SMEs as well as large organisations.

01:13:12.030 --> 01:13:15.690
And that’s where the AHSNs come in.

01:13:15.690 --> 01:13:25.810
Back in 2013, the NHS took the important first
step of creating its own network of local

01:13:25.810 --> 01:13:30.960
innovation agencies, the 15 Academic Health
Science Networks.

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:36.110
No other country’s health service has such
a network.

01:13:36.110 --> 01:13:42.330
From South London to the North East and North
Cumbria, we now have some very impressive

01:13:42.330 --> 01:13:50.480
organisations, and I’ve had the great pleasure
of visiting all 15 and seeing at first hand

01:13:50.480 --> 01:13:57.989
the huge energy of their staff and the impact
that they’re making in their local communities.

01:13:57.989 --> 01:14:07.199
In their first phase, the AHSNs have generated
many brilliant micro examples, supporting

01:14:07.199 --> 01:14:12.620
innovators and beginning to spread these in
their own one fifteenth of the country.

01:14:12.620 --> 01:14:16.130
If I could just highlight one example of that.

01:14:16.130 --> 01:14:17.130
PINCER.

01:14:17.130 --> 01:14:25.300
It’s a method of data analysis of potential
prescribing errors in general practice, allied

01:14:25.300 --> 01:14:28.260
to specific pharmacy training.

01:14:28.260 --> 01:14:34.880
Now, borne out of a trial in Greater Manchester,
the East Midlands AHSN supported the spread

01:14:34.880 --> 01:14:44.050
of PINCER to 361 GP practices right across
the East Midlands, and the result is a 50%

01:14:44.050 --> 01:14:52.760
mean reduction in prescribing errors, and
£2,599 mean savings in prescribing costs

01:14:52.760 --> 01:14:53.940
per practice.

01:14:53.940 --> 01:14:59.460
So, a great result, and why not then go nationwide?

01:14:59.460 --> 01:15:08.630
And with that in mind, last summer, the NHS
England Board posed a challenge to the AHSN

01:15:08.630 --> 01:15:10.090
network.

01:15:10.090 --> 01:15:17.700
We said we would relicense all 15, and we
offered two different versions of their future.

01:15:17.700 --> 01:15:26.090
So, version one was more of the same, with
NHS England funding of £30 million a year.

01:15:26.090 --> 01:15:34.639
And version two was for the individual AHSNs
to work together as a team and develop plans

01:15:34.639 --> 01:15:41.780
to demonstrate collectively higher value-add
for taxpayers and for patients, and we said

01:15:41.780 --> 01:15:45.730
if you do that, we will back you with additional
investment.

01:15:45.730 --> 01:15:53.820
And I am delighted to report today that the
AHSNs have risen to that challenge.

01:15:53.820 --> 01:16:00.840
Of the 226 different innovations that they’ve
been actively backing, they themselves have

01:16:00.840 --> 01:16:06.429
selected seven, which they have all agreed
they will drive nationwide, and Claire Murdoch

01:16:06.429 --> 01:16:11.510
in the earlier session referred to one of
those, the Serenity Integrated mental health

01:16:11.510 --> 01:16:17.429
programme, which is helping to cut the Section
136s.

01:16:17.429 --> 01:16:24.370
The AHSNs, their chief officers, have picked
the innovations that they consider most actionable

01:16:24.370 --> 01:16:31.460
and impactful, and they alone have come up
with their own quantified and phased goals

01:16:31.460 --> 01:16:35.180
on spread, programme by programme.

01:16:35.180 --> 01:16:43.010
And so given the ROI proposed by the AHSNs,
NHS England will now be increasing their funding

01:16:43.010 --> 01:16:47.380
to £44 million a year.

01:16:47.380 --> 01:16:53.590
They have also committed to driving systematic
adoption of the innovation and technology

01:16:53.590 --> 01:16:59.900
tariff and payment products, and any additional
products arising from the accelerated access

01:16:59.900 --> 01:17:06.590
collaborative, as well as creating a national
approach to becoming innovation exchanges

01:17:06.590 --> 01:17:12.000
that support industry throughout the development
process.

01:17:12.000 --> 01:17:20.340
Now, nationwide delivery of these programmes
is just part of the value that they add.

01:17:20.340 --> 01:17:26.330
Where locally commissioned to do so, the AHSNs
also support local service transformation.

01:17:26.330 --> 01:17:34.120
And I would like to conclude by saying I think
that there are three reasons why it really

01:17:34.120 --> 01:17:42.150
matters that AHSNs succeed in delivering against
their public ambitions to spread innovations,

01:17:42.150 --> 01:17:46.460
and that all 15 of them contribute their fair
share.

01:17:46.460 --> 01:17:52.540
The first reason is that each and every programme
they have picked is already proven to deliver

01:17:52.540 --> 01:17:53.940
significant value.

01:17:53.940 --> 01:18:00.320
So, the specific benefits of spreading these
are worth securing.

01:18:00.320 --> 01:18:08.080
Secondly, the programmes they are pursuing
are on one level just illustrations on a bigger

01:18:08.080 --> 01:18:09.740
canvas.

01:18:09.740 --> 01:18:16.460
What the AHSNs will be proving is that they
can support and achieve the spread of proven

01:18:16.460 --> 01:18:24.480
innovations right across the NHS, that different
parts of the NHS can do plagiarism from each

01:18:24.480 --> 01:18:27.980
other, not just invention.

01:18:27.980 --> 01:18:34.240
For med tech, digital and novel diagnostics,
not just for medicines.

01:18:34.240 --> 01:18:47.010
And third and finally, because the NHS needs
a wider set of innovation capabilities that

01:18:47.010 --> 01:18:55.970
won’t just be the strength of the 15 AHSNs
as at 2019 or 2020.

01:18:55.970 --> 01:19:02.590
So, if we look at only delivering against
these new licence goals, it kind of misses

01:19:02.590 --> 01:19:03.760
the point.

01:19:03.760 --> 01:19:12.900
The scale of the AHSNs’ own and our own
long-term ambitions I think needs to be far

01:19:12.900 --> 01:19:22.150
greater, otherwise, the AHSNs will be seen
as very useful and valuable, but not fully

01:19:22.150 --> 01:19:23.300
salient.

01:19:23.300 --> 01:19:29.140
To use your phrase, David, of a second ago:
tributaries rather than the main stream.

01:19:29.140 --> 01:19:35.200
And I think it’s realising that bigger ambition
that is the real prize here.

01:19:35.200 --> 01:19:42.139
So, Malcolm, I would like to commend the paper
and our proposals for AHSN relicensing to

01:19:42.139 --> 01:19:49.750
the Board, and I’d also like to pay tribute
to our first NHS Director of Innovation and

01:19:49.750 --> 01:19:53.510
Life Sciences, Dr Sam Roberts, who’s been
doing a really great job here.

01:19:53.510 --> 01:19:54.510
Thanks.

01:19:54.510 --> 01:19:57.420
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ian, and thank
you for drawing this activity together across

01:19:57.420 --> 01:20:00.530
the country, and we welcome Sam as an addition
to the team.

01:20:00.530 --> 01:20:01.530
Let me open up the paper for questions, please.

01:20:01.530 --> 01:20:04.250
I have Noel Gordon.

01:20:04.250 --> 01:20:05.250
Noel?

01:20:05.250 --> 01:20:10.230
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: One of the characteristics,
Ian, of the Fourth Industrial Revolution,

01:20:10.230 --> 01:20:16.130
unlike the Second and Third Industrial Revolutions,
is you don’t have 50 years to succeed or

01:20:16.130 --> 01:20:18.489
30 years to succeed.

01:20:18.489 --> 01:20:24.740
If you miss the first two years of the Fourth
Industrial Revolution cycles, it’s very

01:20:24.740 --> 01:20:31.190
hard to catch up, so I contextualised AHSNs
in the context of our contribution to life

01:20:31.190 --> 01:20:38.690
sciences, med tech and many of our emerging
industrial strategies around helping companies

01:20:38.690 --> 01:20:45.530
get on the curve in the first two years, because
many of these are Fourth Industrial Revolution

01:20:45.530 --> 01:20:47.000
innovations.

01:20:47.000 --> 01:20:54.389
How do you think the AHSNs are doing over
last year to this year in being able to help

01:20:54.389 --> 01:21:01.630
us in – nurture companies through the first
critical two years of their startup lives?

01:21:01.630 --> 01:21:03.400
Have we got better?

01:21:03.400 --> 01:21:09.150
And if we have got better in the AHSNs at
doing this, what have we done to get better?

01:21:09.150 --> 01:21:13.360
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Exactly so, Noel.

01:21:13.360 --> 01:21:21.690
And I think if you look at the data of the
jobs that they’re creating, over 500 in

01:21:21.690 --> 01:21:24.010
the four years of their licence period.

01:21:24.010 --> 01:21:28.699
If you look at the data on some of the inward
investment they’re generating, and also,

01:21:28.699 --> 01:21:34.250
there’s an annual stakeholder survey, including
from industry, about their perceptions of

01:21:34.250 --> 01:21:41.510
AHSNs, and we can see improvements in perceived
utility of what the AHSNs individually are

01:21:41.510 --> 01:21:42.510
doing.

01:21:42.510 --> 01:21:50.090
As you rightly flag, although the AHSNs have
a critical coordinating rule, they are not

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:55.550
the sum total of all the different activities
that are contributing to the Fourth Industrial

01:21:55.550 --> 01:22:06.750
Revolution, and as the recent House of Lords
enquiry into the life sciences rightly observed,

01:22:06.750 --> 01:22:13.100
there is a huge amount – to pick a theme
for the day – of fragmentation between multiple

01:22:13.100 --> 01:22:20.540
different agencies here, and that includes
around generating proven innovations in the

01:22:20.540 --> 01:22:27.040
first instance, and then actually aligning
the different spread mechanisms, and I’m

01:22:27.040 --> 01:22:34.679
working closely with the Minister, James O’Shaughnessy,
on a review to try and streamline and power

01:22:34.679 --> 01:22:37.100
up that innovation landscape.

01:22:37.100 --> 01:22:40.020
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I have Steve.

01:22:40.020 --> 01:22:41.020
David.

01:22:41.020 --> 01:22:43.530
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Thank you, Ian.

01:22:43.530 --> 01:22:51.909
So, I think the AHSNs are doing and will do
a great job in spreading innovation, but they

01:22:51.909 --> 01:22:53.590
can’t do it on their own.

01:22:53.590 --> 01:22:58.219
And in order to spread innovation and to adopt
innovation and new technologies, they need

01:22:58.219 --> 01:23:03.179
to innovate on the background of a set of
organisations and systems that are fertile

01:23:03.179 --> 01:23:10.210
ground, by which I mean they have the culture,
the tools, the knowledge and the desire to

01:23:10.210 --> 01:23:13.860
take those innovations and take them into
everyday clinical practice.

01:23:13.860 --> 01:23:17.850
And it’s really a comment rather than a
question, so I think one of the things that

01:23:17.850 --> 01:23:23.880
we must do as we bring NHS Improvement and
England together at national level, as we

01:23:23.880 --> 01:23:29.130
develop integrated care systems and, indeed,
as we plan what the NHS should look like over

01:23:29.130 --> 01:23:37.520
the next five to ten years, is to ensure that
we encourage and help and put in place a support

01:23:37.520 --> 01:23:43.810
for our organisations and systems to become
knowledge-based organisations with a learning

01:23:43.810 --> 01:23:50.280
culture that will – that have mechanisms
for adopting that innovation at pace.

01:23:50.280 --> 01:23:56.580
Because otherwise, AHSNs will only go so far,
and it will fall down at the last hurdle,

01:23:56.580 --> 01:23:58.870
which is getting them into everyday practice.

01:23:58.870 --> 01:24:01.850
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I completely agree with that
observation.

01:24:01.850 --> 01:24:07.820
As you say, we need to have very close working,
and we are with NHS Improvement colleagues

01:24:07.820 --> 01:24:14.400
around improvement science and building the
capacity and capabilities, as you say, creating

01:24:14.400 --> 01:24:15.540
the fertile ground.

01:24:15.540 --> 01:24:20.350
I think there’s a variety of different mechanisms
that we will need to power up to support wider

01:24:20.350 --> 01:24:23.610
spread here, of which AHSNs are one part.

01:24:23.610 --> 01:24:29.619
I think there’s more that can be done to
help the Academic Health Science Centres increase

01:24:29.619 --> 01:24:31.560
their individual and collective impact.

01:24:31.560 --> 01:24:35.420
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: You may just want to explain
to those who may not be aware the difference

01:24:35.420 --> 01:24:37.530
between the networks and the centres.

01:24:37.530 --> 01:24:46.520
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, the centres are working out
of basically our – some of our leading tertiary

01:24:46.520 --> 01:24:53.389
institutions in this country, and they’re
collaborations between universities, leading

01:24:53.389 --> 01:25:00.240
teaching hospitals and the NHS, to try and
generate new innovations, and then – that’s

01:25:00.240 --> 01:25:03.550
a simple articulation of it.

01:25:03.550 --> 01:25:14.869
And then the – think of the AHSNs almost
as the distribution network for some 

01:25:14.869 --> 01:25:15.869
of those innovations.

01:25:15.869 --> 01:25:16.869
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: John and David, and Victor.

01:25:16.869 --> 01:25:18.840
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Could I just follow Steve’s
point, actually, and reinforce the great work

01:25:18.840 --> 01:25:20.780
that AHSNs are doing.

01:25:20.780 --> 01:25:26.110
You mentioned INHR, and the thing that really
got that off the ground was the earn-back

01:25:26.110 --> 01:25:30.590
element in the sense that the system, the
money that funded that was set aside, and

01:25:30.590 --> 01:25:34.940
the whole system had to prove that they were
doing the research in order to get their funds

01:25:34.940 --> 01:25:40.900
back, and I wonder whether in order to get
the scale that Steve’s alluding to, we almost

01:25:40.900 --> 01:25:45.940
need to think about some element of that across
the systems, so that once the AHSN network

01:25:45.940 --> 01:25:51.570
has demonstrated that it works, that in addition
to the rest of the AHSNs pushing, we need

01:25:51.570 --> 01:25:56.360
the organisations to be pulling so they can
say, ‘Look, we did adopt it’, in order

01:25:56.360 --> 01:25:59.820
to get the tickbox in their annual budgets.

01:25:59.820 --> 01:26:04.941
Without that, it’s quite difficult for the
AHSNs to push it in to centres that are all

01:26:04.941 --> 01:26:07.320
struggling to balance their books.

01:26:07.320 --> 01:26:11.980
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Completely agree with you, John.

01:26:11.980 --> 01:26:21.909
And just an observation is that in 2008, Lord
Ara Darzi in his report proposed the creation

01:26:21.909 --> 01:26:27.400
of this incentive called CQUIN, which stands
for Commissioning for Quality and Innovation.

01:26:27.400 --> 01:26:33.590
Arguably, the ‘I’ in CQUIN has been relatively
silent up until now, and I think that’s

01:26:33.590 --> 01:26:36.960
something that we should consider further.

01:26:36.960 --> 01:26:47.280
I’d also point though to not just the wider
incentives that we can drive in the NHS ecosystem,

01:26:47.280 --> 01:26:52.889
but also the things we can do to help accelerate
the development of different provider models.

01:26:52.889 --> 01:26:58.389
Many of you round this Board will have seen
the fantastic work that Moorfields are doing

01:26:58.389 --> 01:27:03.139
with DeepMind on applications of machine learning
and AI.

01:27:03.139 --> 01:27:09.700
Now, Moorfields happens to run 32 different
satellite ophthalmology departments; you have

01:27:09.700 --> 01:27:11.140
‘Moorfields At’.

01:27:11.140 --> 01:27:17.750
And so if they crack an innovative method
in the mother ship, they have a governance

01:27:17.750 --> 01:27:23.480
mechanism to be able to spread that much more
rapidly across the NHS, and I think that approach

01:27:23.480 --> 01:27:24.830
is potentially very promising.

01:27:24.830 --> 01:27:26.530
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: David and Victor.

01:27:26.530 --> 01:27:31.270
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think Stephen and John
have actually pretty much covered my point,

01:27:31.270 --> 01:27:41.719
which is about – it’s very encouraging
to see innovation, but I get very frustrated

01:27:41.719 --> 01:27:47.449
about the speed at which innovation is then
turned into – to use that word again – the

01:27:47.449 --> 01:27:48.449
mainstream.

01:27:48.449 --> 01:27:54.350
And about – this is about how we implement,
it’s about the culture that we have, and

01:27:54.350 --> 01:27:55.889
it’s about the decision rights.

01:27:55.889 --> 01:28:00.949
And therefore, I’ve just – no need to
respond, we’ve had this conversation – but

01:28:00.949 --> 01:28:07.630
I’d really encourage us to think about how
we scale some of these things quickly, what’s

01:28:07.630 --> 01:28:13.650
the level of proof that we need to have before
we make a decision?

01:28:13.650 --> 01:28:18.320
Because some of the people I’ve spoken to
in the networks would observe that they spend

01:28:18.320 --> 01:28:24.320
all their time proving and answering the marginal
question when they’ve proven the main point.

01:28:24.320 --> 01:28:30.000
So, I think there’s something about how
we take in – how we think about acceleration,

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:32.219
because Noel’s point was absolutely right.

01:28:32.219 --> 01:28:33.780
We have to get these innovations out quickly.

01:28:33.780 --> 01:28:39.770
There are innovations that we saw at the Board
last year that would save kids’ lives that

01:28:39.770 --> 01:28:45.640
are not mainstream, and I think that’s shocking.

01:28:45.640 --> 01:28:54.050
So, I think diffusion and adoption and implementation,
we need to put more focus on that, or as much

01:28:54.050 --> 01:28:56.679
focus, as we are on the innovation itself.

01:28:56.679 --> 01:29:00.330
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Can I just draw attention
to one of the items that’s in the paper,

01:29:00.330 --> 01:29:04.989
which is PRECEPT, which takes this point exactly.

01:29:04.989 --> 01:29:09.580
Reduces the risk of cerebral palsy by 30%
in babies born to 11,000 mothers per year

01:29:09.580 --> 01:29:14.790
who go into pre-term labour, ties directly
back into the presentation we’ve just had

01:29:14.790 --> 01:29:17.090
on maternity transformation.

01:29:17.090 --> 01:29:21.920
And I think the Board are really interested
to understand – but maybe not for you to

01:29:21.920 --> 01:29:26.810
answer now, Ian – but what is it that we
in combination with NHS Improvement might

01:29:26.810 --> 01:29:33.619
be able to do to foster the quicker adoption
of things which are of such critical importance

01:29:33.619 --> 01:29:36.750
to such a vulnerable population?

01:29:36.750 --> 01:29:38.489
May we just park that one?

01:29:38.489 --> 01:29:39.489
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Exactly.

01:29:39.489 --> 01:29:45.869
We have now figures by AHSN about how they’re
going to drive this across 33% of the country,

01:29:45.869 --> 01:29:50.040
bearing in mind some of the constraints that
have already been alluded to.

01:29:50.040 --> 01:29:52.540
That’s why the number isn’t higher.

01:29:52.540 --> 01:29:58.300
But collectively, if actually, we think 33%
if the right answer for something that costs

01:29:58.300 --> 01:30:04.320
£1 a vial for magnesium sulphate, then clearly,
you know, that’s the wrong answer, which

01:30:04.320 --> 01:30:09.110
is my – last point of my opening comments
around, this can’t be the sum of our ambitions.

01:30:09.110 --> 01:30:14.710
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: We should set the target
at 80% or 90% or 100%, and work out how we’re

01:30:14.710 --> 01:30:17.330
going to do it on things like this.

01:30:17.330 --> 01:30:21.840
Would be where I would come from if – if
I’m in my other world, if I see an innovation

01:30:21.840 --> 01:30:25.469
that’s going to make a massive difference,
that’s going to save money, but crucially,

01:30:25.469 --> 01:30:30.619
is going to make a better outcome for patients,
for parents, in this case, and everyone, then

01:30:30.619 --> 01:30:35.150
we’d be asking ourselves, how can we get
that out to 100% of the population in the

01:30:35.150 --> 01:30:36.290
next three months?

01:30:36.290 --> 01:30:39.150
Not the approach we’ve got at the moment.

01:30:39.150 --> 01:30:41.150
Sorry to get cross about it; I just feel very
–

01:30:41.150 --> 01:30:43.480
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: No, no, you’re not.

01:30:43.480 --> 01:30:45.430
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: – annoyed about it.

01:30:45.430 --> 01:30:50.490
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So, parents are entitled
to ask under those circumstances, why was

01:30:50.490 --> 01:30:52.850
this not available for my child?

01:30:52.850 --> 01:30:53.850
Victor?

01:30:53.850 --> 01:30:56.460
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: This might echo David’s
point, which I agree with.

01:30:56.460 --> 01:31:02.170
You talk about ROI as a measure, and I wonder
whether we are measuring – well, could you

01:31:02.170 --> 01:31:04.719
say something about how we measure value?

01:31:04.719 --> 01:31:10.340
Because that might help us get to David’s
point, given that –

01:31:10.340 --> 01:31:14.650
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, we use ROI as a necessary
discipline.

01:31:14.650 --> 01:31:19.909
But if you think back to our triple aim, it’s
about better health and wellbeing, it’s

01:31:19.909 --> 01:31:22.390
about better quality of care for patients.

01:31:22.390 --> 01:31:28.440
And if you just take some of these individual
programmes, so I’ve touched on the benefits

01:31:28.440 --> 01:31:36.389
of PINCER, not just in terms of prescribing
failings, but cutting by 50% prescribing errors

01:31:36.389 --> 01:31:38.699
in general practice, so a big safety benefit.

01:31:38.699 --> 01:31:46.969
On AF, the benefit here is reducing strokes
and saving lives.

01:31:46.969 --> 01:31:54.780
Claire Murdoch’s already talked about Serenity
Integrated Mentoring in London, which is about

01:31:54.780 --> 01:31:59.850
improving health and wellbeing, wider social
benefits, including to other public services

01:31:59.850 --> 01:32:02.030
such as the police.

01:32:02.030 --> 01:32:10.010
And on PRECEPT, we’ve talked about actually
saving avoidable cerebral palsy births.

01:32:10.010 --> 01:32:15.110
So, we will have – and one of the things
I think we will have for the first time as

01:32:15.110 --> 01:32:20.110
a result of the work we’ve been doing with
the AHSNs is, when they produce their annual

01:32:20.110 --> 01:32:26.260
report in a year’s time, we won’t end
up, as we’ve had in the first five years

01:32:26.260 --> 01:32:31.679
of their existence, with a collection of individual
case studies about what they’ve done.

01:32:31.679 --> 01:32:38.060
Instead, we will be able to point to collective
impacts, not just in terms of savings for

01:32:38.060 --> 01:32:40.429
the NHS, but actually around health and wellbeing,
and around quality of care.

01:32:40.429 --> 01:32:41.429
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: David, do you want to add
–

01:32:41.429 --> 01:32:49.619
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I just want to make clear
my frustration is not with the AHSNs.

01:32:49.619 --> 01:32:54.900
My frustration is actually with the system
and – which is represented by the boards,

01:32:54.900 --> 01:32:56.960
about our failure to diffuse.

01:32:56.960 --> 01:33:00.730
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay, I think that’s a
valuable clarification.

01:33:00.730 --> 01:33:02.800
I think a couple of comments.

01:33:02.800 --> 01:33:06.710
First of all, the Board has agreed through
the Investment Committee to increase the investment

01:33:06.710 --> 01:33:12.750
in AHSNs, and that I think is a decision which
is well supported by what we’ve heard today.

01:33:12.750 --> 01:33:16.489
And David’s last point, which is that, you
know, we think the AHSNs are actually doing

01:33:16.489 --> 01:33:18.159
a really good job across the country.

01:33:18.159 --> 01:33:22.510
They’re doing a necessary job, they’re
getting the intersection between industry,

01:33:22.510 --> 01:33:26.800
NHS and academia; that seems to be coming
on well.

01:33:26.800 --> 01:33:31.690
I think secondly, the Board’s responsibility
which comes back to us is, how do we get better

01:33:31.690 --> 01:33:37.270
adoption and how do we – and this is a constant
refrain across the NHS from time immemorial,

01:33:37.270 --> 01:33:42.429
but one that’s very frustrating, when we
can see that our own teams, on our investment

01:33:42.429 --> 01:33:48.610
and generating invention and discovery, and
yet the system is not sucking it in and making

01:33:48.610 --> 01:33:52.560
use of it, and it’s not just the economic
consequences; it’s the consequences for

01:33:52.560 --> 01:33:56.889
safety, for patients, that comes strikingly
out of the paper.

01:33:56.889 --> 01:34:00.969
So, Ian, actually, full thanks to you for
drawing all of this together.

01:34:00.969 --> 01:34:02.200
But could I just stress your last point.

01:34:02.200 --> 01:34:06.119
When we come to look at this again, it would
be really good to look at what the network

01:34:06.119 --> 01:34:10.369
has achieved, as opposed to individual case
studies.

01:34:10.369 --> 01:34:13.040
Great, thank you very much.

01:34:13.040 --> 01:34:17.780
May we move on then to developing the health
inequalities action plan.

01:34:17.780 --> 01:34:24.480
And this is going to be with Steve and Jane,
and Neil Churchill is joining us, as well.

01:34:24.480 --> 01:34:33.739
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: So, thank you, Malcolm, and
I will be brief in my introduction, but firstly,

01:34:33.739 --> 01:34:34.787
to welcome Neil Churchill to the table, who’s
our Director for Experience, Participation

01:34:34.787 --> 01:34:35.787
and Equalities.

01:34:35.787 --> 01:34:40.369
So, the Board will recall that at the last
Board meeting, we discussed a paper on health

01:34:40.369 --> 01:34:45.699
inequalities, which laid out the evidence
base – the current evidence base for inequalities

01:34:45.699 --> 01:34:52.119
and at a broad level described the approach
that we’re taking at NHS England to address

01:34:52.119 --> 01:34:54.460
and reduce health inequalities.

01:34:54.460 --> 01:34:58.890
And the Board asked after that discussion
that we brought back to this Board a more

01:34:58.890 --> 01:35:03.659
detailed description of the specific actions
that are being taken to address inequalities

01:35:03.659 --> 01:35:08.210
in our key programmes, and indeed, we’ve
had the opportunity this morning to discuss

01:35:08.210 --> 01:35:14.340
that with Sarah-Jane and Claire, who of course
lead two of our key programmes, and it’s

01:35:14.340 --> 01:35:18.310
been a welcome opportunity to discuss that
in a bit more detail.

01:35:18.310 --> 01:35:21.739
So, that is what we have before you today.

01:35:21.739 --> 01:35:28.300
This, in a sense, is an interim paper, and
in the paper, there is also a series of next

01:35:28.300 --> 01:35:30.320
steps, and I will just highlight a few of
those.

01:35:30.320 --> 01:35:38.630
So, one is to ensure that as we go forward,
that we not only survey our existing programmes

01:35:38.630 --> 01:35:44.060
to determine what they are doing in terms
of – or the actions they’re taking that

01:35:44.060 --> 01:35:50.780
might impact on inequalities, but increasingly
to design into them specific programmes that

01:35:50.780 --> 01:35:54.050
will actively target a reduction in inequalities.

01:35:54.050 --> 01:35:57.670
That is occurring in some of our programmes;
we need to focus on that more.

01:35:57.670 --> 01:36:02.489
And secondly, to ensure, as we discussed earlier
with Claire and Sarah-Jane, that we understand

01:36:02.489 --> 01:36:09.699
the measures that we need, the metrics that
we need to develop, to track changes in health

01:36:09.699 --> 01:36:14.550
inequalities that – especially in those
areas, of course, that we’re targeting,

01:36:14.550 --> 01:36:18.409
and Matthew’s team is working with us on
that.

01:36:18.409 --> 01:36:25.949
So, I will leave it there, other than to ask
Jane and Neil whether they want to add anything.

01:36:25.949 --> 01:36:31.480
We will come back later in the year with a
more detailed update on this, but Jane and

01:36:31.480 --> 01:36:32.719
Neil, anything you wish to add?

01:36:32.719 --> 01:36:35.679
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Well, I certainly don’t,
because Neil’s been leading this programme,

01:36:35.679 --> 01:36:38.270
so I thought it would be worth handing over
to Neil.

01:36:38.270 --> 01:36:39.330
So, thank you, Steve.

01:36:39.330 --> 01:36:40.909
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Okay, thank you, Jane.

01:36:40.909 --> 01:36:43.800
So, I think Steve’s covered most of the
key points.

01:36:43.800 --> 01:36:47.369
Just to say that we heard some great presentations
earlier.

01:36:47.369 --> 01:36:52.599
I think we’re also minded that we can’t
assume that a generic approach will by itself

01:36:52.599 --> 01:36:57.250
reduce inequalities, and so we’ve been very
keen to make sure that we understand the specific

01:36:57.250 --> 01:37:01.179
needs of different groups in all of the programmes.

01:37:01.179 --> 01:37:05.010
What you asked for last time was a sense of,
what are the programmes doing, for whom, and

01:37:05.010 --> 01:37:07.360
how we’re going to measure it.

01:37:07.360 --> 01:37:11.840
I think we’ve made quite a lot of progress
in clarifying what the programmes are doing

01:37:11.840 --> 01:37:12.840
and for whom.

01:37:12.840 --> 01:37:17.700
And as you can see, I think where there’s
work in progress, I think there’s a lot

01:37:17.700 --> 01:37:22.010
of it around the measurement, and so there’s
been I think quite a lot of thought in the

01:37:22.010 --> 01:37:25.420
programmes about how they can improve the
measurements, how they can start to use measurements

01:37:25.420 --> 01:37:30.199
that are coming online, and how they can fill
some of the gaps in the measurement, and people

01:37:30.199 --> 01:37:34.469
have either had deep dives in the programmes
since the last Board meeting, or they are

01:37:34.469 --> 01:37:35.510
planning to do so.

01:37:35.510 --> 01:37:38.630
So, there’s a lot more detail that sits
underneath this, but we didn’t want to table

01:37:38.630 --> 01:37:42.580
a very long paper, so this gives you a flavour
of what’s happening.

01:37:42.580 --> 01:37:46.760
It recognises there’s more to do, and I
think particularly more to do cross cutting,

01:37:46.760 --> 01:37:49.190
across the different programmes, so that we
join those up.

01:37:49.190 --> 01:37:51.510
But very happy to take questions and comments.

01:37:51.510 --> 01:37:54.170
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, thank you, Neil, and
thank you for your work on this.

01:37:54.170 --> 01:37:59.380
I think it’s evidence that the Board’s
strong intent in relation to health inequalities

01:37:59.380 --> 01:38:01.760
has been picked up by the team.

01:38:01.760 --> 01:38:04.330
This is an interim paper.

01:38:04.330 --> 01:38:07.179
Greater detail will come back to us in the
autumn.

01:38:07.179 --> 01:38:11.119
The question for the Board is whether they
take sufficient assurance from the paper that

01:38:11.119 --> 01:38:12.810
work is indeed in hand.

01:38:12.810 --> 01:38:22.550
On that basis, may I invite any questions
or points to put to the team?

01:38:22.550 --> 01:38:23.550
Joanne?

01:38:23.550 --> 01:38:24.550
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you, Malcolm.

01:38:24.550 --> 01:38:26.040
In answer to your question about, does this
feel like the right sort of thing, going in

01:38:26.040 --> 01:38:33.590
the right sort of direction, I think we’ve
had a very sort of positive response to that.

01:38:33.590 --> 01:38:36.239
I’d like to make a couple of sort of conditional
points, if I can.

01:38:36.239 --> 01:38:42.600
One was, you’ve got a cautious paragraph,
Neil, on page four about where appropriate,

01:38:42.600 --> 01:38:45.980
programmes reviewing their approach to segmentation.

01:38:45.980 --> 01:38:53.420
And I really endorse that, and I’d encourage
programmes through you to be – take quite

01:38:53.420 --> 01:38:59.760
an open-minded, creative, inventive, bold
approach to looking at, and what different

01:38:59.760 --> 01:39:05.380
approaches could make a real impact, including
things like incentives, and greater targeting.

01:39:05.380 --> 01:39:09.400
So, I can see, you know, you’re thinking
in that direction, and I’d like to kind

01:39:09.400 --> 01:39:11.621
of give that a bit of encouragement.

01:39:11.621 --> 01:39:15.599
And the other thing that I’d perhaps like
to see over the longer term is, in the same

01:39:15.599 --> 01:39:20.560
way that our national programme reports now
talk about public and patient involvement,

01:39:20.560 --> 01:39:27.830
you know, maybe in the future, national programme
reports will talk specifically about what

01:39:27.830 --> 01:39:29.090
they’re doing to reduce inequality.

01:39:29.090 --> 01:39:35.090
Obviously, you know, being able to measure
that is a key part of it, but fantastic, and

01:39:35.090 --> 01:39:37.350
thank you for bringing it to us today.

01:39:37.350 --> 01:39:39.489
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

01:39:39.489 --> 01:39:40.489
Right.

01:39:40.489 --> 01:39:41.630
Oh, Victor, please.

01:39:41.630 --> 01:39:43.770
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Thank you.

01:39:43.770 --> 01:39:45.369
This is progress.

01:39:45.369 --> 01:39:49.929
It’s very good, the three people who’ve
been involved in this.

01:39:49.929 --> 01:39:56.739
I would – so this is progress in relation
to the reference to specific programmes, and

01:39:56.739 --> 01:39:58.750
I particularly like the sight of specific
activities.

01:39:58.750 --> 01:40:07.760
The challenge is, as Joanne referred to, to
make this core business, in the sense of…

01:40:07.760 --> 01:40:14.170
So, I guess I just want to confirm – and
I suppose I’m looking for Simon on – that

01:40:14.170 --> 01:40:21.270
that is the direction of travel, that we are
pushing this down, this is going to be core

01:40:21.270 --> 01:40:24.620
business, it’s going to be part of what
happens in the Next Steps, it’s going to

01:40:24.620 --> 01:40:28.300
be an expectation of programme design as well
as programme outcome.

01:40:28.300 --> 01:40:35.650
Frankly, virtually everything we’ve discussed
on the agenda so far is riddled with an opportunity

01:40:35.650 --> 01:40:42.900
to do that, so that’s where I’m at.

01:40:42.900 --> 01:40:46.619
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: So, I think the answer to
that is, yes, Victor, as we think about the

01:40:46.619 --> 01:40:51.639
next five and ten years, we want to be intentional
about building in prospectively inequalities

01:40:51.639 --> 01:40:58.050
reductions, goals, as part of the programme
that we agree with our partners there.

01:40:58.050 --> 01:41:01.390
And in the meantime, obviously there’s the
work that’s described in this paper, but

01:41:01.390 --> 01:41:06.020
there are the broader determinants of inequalities,
and we’ve got to make sure we have an inequalities

01:41:06.020 --> 01:41:07.349
lens in each of those.

01:41:07.349 --> 01:41:13.449
So, for example, the discussion which there
has been even in this week about the approach

01:41:13.449 --> 01:41:15.489
to clean air.

01:41:15.489 --> 01:41:20.790
We know that in terms of airborne pollution
and the impact on respiratory admissions,

01:41:20.790 --> 01:41:27.030
particularly for children from poorer families,
there’s an inequalities lens there.

01:41:27.030 --> 01:41:34.449
We know that the reinvigorated childhood obesity
strategy will have a particular benefit if

01:41:34.449 --> 01:41:39.869
we get it right for children from poorer families.

01:41:39.869 --> 01:41:45.199
For example, the single largest reason why
children are admitted to hospital is to have

01:41:45.199 --> 01:41:47.679
their rotten teeth removed.

01:41:47.679 --> 01:41:51.240
That affects poorer children more.

01:41:51.240 --> 01:41:56.790
If we have childhood obesity policies with
some teeth, then our poorer children will

01:41:56.790 --> 01:41:59.460
get to keep their own teeth.

01:41:59.460 --> 01:42:04.639
So, all of these public policies need to be
refracted through the lens of the impact they

01:42:04.639 --> 01:42:09.580
will have on the disadvantaged amongst our
society.

01:42:09.580 --> 01:42:16.949
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I just wonder, if I may,
the NHSE, NHSI coming together, whether this

01:42:16.949 --> 01:42:20.889
is an issue that challenges both organisations
within the authorities.

01:42:20.889 --> 01:42:26.389
I appreciate your point made about the wider
determinants, but I’m quite focused on what

01:42:26.389 --> 01:42:30.600
we can do within the authority that we’ve
got, and that’s about to change as NHSE

01:42:30.600 --> 01:42:32.230
comes together with NHSI.

01:42:32.230 --> 01:42:38.360
So, I’m curious about whether this is something
that we intend to take forward in our discussion

01:42:38.360 --> 01:42:39.420
with them.

01:42:39.420 --> 01:42:44.530
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: We have that opportunity
in our joint Board meeting this very afternoon.

01:42:44.530 --> 01:42:45.530
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Excellent.

01:42:45.530 --> 01:42:46.900
I’m sure we’ll take it up.

01:42:46.900 --> 01:42:47.900
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Jane?

01:42:47.900 --> 01:42:53.750
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: So, I just wanted to respond
to Victor’s question by really just identifying,

01:42:53.750 --> 01:42:58.550
as we’ve heard today, that actually, this
was a lens through which we looked at the

01:42:58.550 --> 01:43:03.429
maternity programme from the very beginning,
and in a similar way to the learning disability

01:43:03.429 --> 01:43:07.440
programme, and there are – yes, there is
more we can do, but it was a very specific

01:43:07.440 --> 01:43:09.760
focus in that.

01:43:09.760 --> 01:43:16.139
And then with my regional director for London
hat on, this work is incredibly important

01:43:16.139 --> 01:43:17.139
across London.

01:43:17.139 --> 01:43:21.710
It’s part of the London partnership work,
it’s part of our devolution, and it’s

01:43:21.710 --> 01:43:26.429
actually working with our STPs, with the GLA,
with the local boroughs, and actually, things

01:43:26.429 --> 01:43:32.730
like childhood obesity, air pollution, are
massive priorities as – just for two – across

01:43:32.730 --> 01:43:36.500
London, and we’ve got a really specific
piece of work that we’re doing as part of

01:43:36.500 --> 01:43:40.210
the Mayor’s inequalities strategy to really
improve health across London.

01:43:40.210 --> 01:43:44.510
And I think this is a really good opportunity
for us to work collectively and to deliver

01:43:44.510 --> 01:43:45.510
that.

01:43:45.510 --> 01:43:46.510
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good.

01:43:46.510 --> 01:43:47.760
Well, thank you, Neil, Jane and Steve.

01:43:47.760 --> 01:43:51.750
The Board – I’m sorry, you want to come
in, Michelle.

01:43:51.750 --> 01:43:55.770
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Just very briefly, thank
you for all your work in this area.

01:43:55.770 --> 01:43:56.900
I wonder, given it’s an action plan, whether
we could bring it back in a year’s time

01:43:56.900 --> 01:44:00.800
to see the progress that has been made.

01:44:00.800 --> 01:44:07.830
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, we’re coming back
in the autumn, aren’t we, with more detail

01:44:07.830 --> 01:44:11.170
on the action plan, and then I’m sure the
Board will want to chase down the progress.

01:44:11.170 --> 01:44:12.170
Good.

01:44:12.170 --> 01:44:16.330
Okay, thank you very much, and I think this
is a major step forward.

01:44:16.330 --> 01:44:23.840
I think Simon’s outline of how health inequalities
gets embedded in everything that we do is

01:44:23.840 --> 01:44:24.949
absolutely compelling.

01:44:24.949 --> 01:44:25.949
Right.

01:44:25.949 --> 01:44:27.179
Matthew, you’re on.

01:44:27.179 --> 01:44:33.200
NHS performance and progress on implementation
of the Next Steps on the NHS Five Year Forward

01:44:33.200 --> 01:44:34.200
View.

01:44:34.200 --> 01:44:35.870
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you, Chair.

01:44:35.870 --> 01:44:37.350
I – people have got the reports.

01:44:37.350 --> 01:44:43.239
I’d just like to pull out a small number
of highlights, I think, that are worth mentioning.

01:44:43.239 --> 01:44:51.929
I think the first one is in paragraph 4, where
what you can see is the combined impact of

01:44:51.929 --> 01:44:58.320
partnership working across all parts of the
NHS has enabled us to get more patients cared

01:44:58.320 --> 01:45:02.489
for in the right places than we have seen
previously.

01:45:02.489 --> 01:45:09.420
So, you can see that the actual number of
GP referrals into hospital fell by 1.6% last

01:45:09.420 --> 01:45:11.210
year compared to the year before.

01:45:11.210 --> 01:45:16.610
That’s against a four-year average growth
of 3.8% per year.

01:45:16.610 --> 01:45:23.510
A&amp;E attendances grew by 2.4% against a 4%
four-year average, and the A&amp;E attendances

01:45:23.510 --> 01:45:32.010
in type 1 hospital A&amp;Es only grew by 0.8%,
which is a sign that the urgent treatment

01:45:32.010 --> 01:45:37.680
centres, walk-in centres, minor injury units
and the extended access, increasingly often

01:45:37.680 --> 01:45:41.730
across primary care, is keeping patients away
from A&amp;Es when they don’t need to be there

01:45:41.730 --> 01:45:46.310
and treating them in more appropriate locations.

01:45:46.310 --> 01:45:48.760
You can see it in the emergency admissions.

01:45:48.760 --> 01:45:57.170
Overnight admissions only rose by 1.7% last
year for emergencies, whereas emergency day

01:45:57.170 --> 01:46:02.450
cases, which we’re seeing increasingly patients
cared for and treated in A&amp;Es and then discharged

01:46:02.450 --> 01:46:10.550
in – without an overnight stay, grew by
7.2%, so that’s a sign that new models,

01:46:10.550 --> 01:46:15.489
new ways of working in A&amp;Es for conditions
are being developed.

01:46:15.489 --> 01:46:24.020
The upshot of which was that the pressure
on hospital beds only grew by 0.5% last year,

01:46:24.020 --> 01:46:27.060
which is, again, well below the annual average.

01:46:27.060 --> 01:46:33.349
When we see what’s happened to bed occupancy
and the numbers that have been published today

01:46:33.349 --> 01:46:39.000
on bed occupancy, you can see that in many
parts of the country, the number of beds available

01:46:39.000 --> 01:46:42.440
actually shrank last year, and if it hadn’t
been for that work to keep people away from

01:46:42.440 --> 01:46:48.790
being admitted, we would have seen even greater
pressure on the NHS.

01:46:48.790 --> 01:46:53.969
We’ve seen over winter, which happily has
now come to an end, finally, I can declare

01:46:53.969 --> 01:46:56.440
at this Board meeting that we are no longer
in winter, that –

01:46:56.440 --> 01:46:59.980
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: We’ve got a Bank Holiday
weekend –

01:46:59.980 --> 01:47:10.719
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: That means the weather is –
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: – that the – that

01:47:10.719 --> 01:47:15.510
we saw over winter the highest levels of flu
that we’d seen for six years, and we saw

01:47:15.510 --> 01:47:21.670
a spike in norovirus impacting on the NHS,
which did impact on A&amp;E performance.

01:47:21.670 --> 01:47:28.699
I’m pleased that in the past month, A&amp;E
performance recovered back to 88.5%, which

01:47:28.699 --> 01:47:34.610
is a 4% increase in performance on the four-hour
wait on the previous month, and we are still

01:47:34.610 --> 01:47:39.489
working to get it back above 90%, and – but
looking at last month, we are now – the

01:47:39.489 --> 01:47:44.719
number of hospitals that are hitting the 95%
standard is increasing significantly, and

01:47:44.719 --> 01:47:45.869
across the country.

01:47:45.869 --> 01:47:50.871
And particular congratulations, I think, to
Cornwall Hospital, which hit the 95% standard

01:47:50.871 --> 01:47:57.050
for the first time in certainly I can remember,
so tremendous work that they’ve done down

01:47:57.050 --> 01:48:00.630
there to turn that round.

01:48:00.630 --> 01:48:06.340
Across our urgent and emergency care strategy
more widely, we’ve now had every one of

01:48:06.340 --> 01:48:14.090
our major A&amp;Es has got primary care frontdoor
streaming in place, allowing us to separate

01:48:14.090 --> 01:48:19.000
patients who need primary care from the patients
who can be – who really need to be in the

01:48:19.000 --> 01:48:21.130
A&amp;E.

01:48:21.130 --> 01:48:25.550
Between ourselves – between the NHS and
working with our colleagues in local government,

01:48:25.550 --> 01:48:30.630
we reduced the number of delayed transfers
of care by nearly 1,700 beds, which was a

01:48:30.630 --> 01:48:34.800
tremendous effort during the year.

01:48:34.800 --> 01:48:41.840
And across primary care, we are able to track
now additional investment of £171 million

01:48:41.840 --> 01:48:48.530
into primary care, and are on track to hit
our investment target.

01:48:48.530 --> 01:48:55.840
55% of the country now has access to extended
primary care evenings and weekend services

01:48:55.840 --> 01:49:01.900
seven days a week; that includes the whole
of London now has access to extended primary

01:49:01.900 --> 01:49:02.900
care.

01:49:02.900 --> 01:49:06.989
It’s ahead of our target for now, which
was to have been at 40% for the country, and

01:49:06.989 --> 01:49:12.880
we are on track to hit the whole of the country
by the end of October, which is bringing forward

01:49:12.880 --> 01:49:17.550
the ambition from the end of this year in
order to try and get extended access in place

01:49:17.550 --> 01:49:22.410
across the country before we go into next
winter.

01:49:22.410 --> 01:49:28.130
And recognising the pressure that GP services
are under, our international recruitment programme

01:49:28.130 --> 01:49:34.890
is now up and running and – to try and recruit
2,000 GPs into the NHS, and we are hoping

01:49:34.890 --> 01:49:38.850
that the first candidates will actually be
starting working in the NHS around September

01:49:38.850 --> 01:49:40.119
this year.

01:49:40.119 --> 01:49:43.260
So, that work is rolling on.

01:49:43.260 --> 01:49:46.290
We’ve talked about mental health, so I won’t
go back to that.

01:49:46.290 --> 01:49:54.530
But I would just like to mention the work
that’s been going on around information

01:49:54.530 --> 01:49:58.370
technology and our digital agenda, as it’s
come up a number of times today.

01:49:58.370 --> 01:50:06.540
It’s picked up in paragraph 30, but I think
it is worth noting that we now have 14 million

01:50:06.540 --> 01:50:12.510
people across the country registered to Patient
Online, able to access their medical records

01:50:12.510 --> 01:50:19.810
online, be able to book a GP appointment,
change a GP appointment, access repeat prescriptions,

01:50:19.810 --> 01:50:23.949
and that is a rapid take-off that we are now
seeing.

01:50:23.949 --> 01:50:30.090
And we have free WiFi available in 5,000 GP
practices, and we’re in the process of rolling

01:50:30.090 --> 01:50:32.239
that out across the country.

01:50:32.239 --> 01:50:39.230
And the NHS digital apps library that was
mentioned both in the maternity and the mental

01:50:39.230 --> 01:50:46.000
health discussions, we’ve got – we now
have 47 health applications that have been

01:50:46.000 --> 01:50:51.740
screened by NHS Digital and are available
through the library, and 80,000 people being

01:50:51.740 --> 01:50:57.500
supported in the use of digital health technologies
through the NHS’s widening digital participation

01:50:57.500 --> 01:51:03.830
programme, identify – at getting people
who are not natural users of iPhone apps to

01:51:03.830 --> 01:51:08.889
take advantage of the technologies that we’re
making available.

01:51:08.889 --> 01:51:17.480
We announced earlier this week the first cohort
of our local health and care record exemplars,

01:51:17.480 --> 01:51:24.070
the first three areas being London, Manchester
and Wessex, I think a term that only the NHS

01:51:24.070 --> 01:51:25.909
still uses.

01:51:25.909 --> 01:51:29.199
But will give us –
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: And Hardy.

01:51:29.199 --> 01:51:35.239
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, absolutely, we are
– we will be implementing Hardy’s books…

01:51:35.239 --> 01:51:39.139
The – I think it’s a very exciting development.

01:51:39.139 --> 01:51:44.409
We have been investing in provider digitalisation,
so hospitals, in mental health, in primary

01:51:44.409 --> 01:51:49.719
care, community services, and this is the
moment when we start to bring that information

01:51:49.719 --> 01:51:55.489
on the patient together, along with social
care information, to support this – the

01:51:55.489 --> 01:51:59.889
care provider for patients, so that clinicians,
whatever they’re setting, can see all the

01:51:59.889 --> 01:52:02.929
information that the NHS has on them in order
to be able to make sure their medications

01:52:02.929 --> 01:52:08.320
are right, they’re getting the right treatment,
and that we are able to help manage their

01:52:08.320 --> 01:52:10.469
care through a pathway.

01:52:10.469 --> 01:52:13.820
I think that is a significant step forward
for us.

01:52:13.820 --> 01:52:17.710
We will be looking – we’re looking to
find two more areas for our exemplars, and

01:52:17.710 --> 01:52:22.340
we are looking over the next few years to
roll that out across the country, so that

01:52:22.340 --> 01:52:26.400
wherever a patient touches the NHS or social
care, the information that a professional

01:52:26.400 --> 01:52:31.880
needs in order to give you the best care possible
is available.

01:52:31.880 --> 01:52:40.770
And the amount of enthusiasm that this has
generated across the NHS, led by local clinicians,

01:52:40.770 --> 01:52:42.159
has been overwhelming.

01:52:42.159 --> 01:52:46.659
We – these three areas will cover about
14 million of the population.

01:52:46.659 --> 01:52:51.159
We had bids covering 40 million of the population,
so almost the whole of the country has responded

01:52:51.159 --> 01:52:57.849
to this, and has I think brought together
a conversation around – between clinicians

01:52:57.849 --> 01:53:01.960
about the sharing of data and the need to
do that to improve quality, which I – and

01:53:01.960 --> 01:53:06.929
I think is absolutely crucial to the points
that Stephen and David were raising about,

01:53:06.929 --> 01:53:09.380
how do you roll innovation out more quickly.

01:53:09.380 --> 01:53:14.070
You can only really do that when we’re sharing
the same information, you have the same view

01:53:14.070 --> 01:53:15.070
on the patient.

01:53:15.070 --> 01:53:17.070
Then you can standardise the roll-out of best
practice.

01:53:17.070 --> 01:53:19.909
So, I think that’s exciting.

01:53:19.909 --> 01:53:24.000
We will be moving now – because we’ve
just exited one winter – into our planning

01:53:24.000 --> 01:53:29.100
for the coming year and what needs to be done,
and I think the crucial part of making the

01:53:29.100 --> 01:53:34.940
coming year work, whether it’s around maintaining
our waiting lists, improving our A&amp;E or preparing

01:53:34.940 --> 01:53:39.710
for next winter, is creating the capacity
that the NHS needs in order to be able to

01:53:39.710 --> 01:53:43.469
get patients through hospitals.

01:53:43.469 --> 01:53:49.410
Between ourselves and NHSI, we are estimating
that the NHS needs about another 4,000 beds’

01:53:49.410 --> 01:53:51.390
worth of capacity.

01:53:51.390 --> 01:53:56.239
If you look at the patients who are in hospital
for more than three weeks, some of them are

01:53:56.239 --> 01:54:00.190
appropriately in hospital for more than three
weeks: they’ve had complex operations, they’ve

01:54:00.190 --> 01:54:03.550
got infections that take a while to recover
from.

01:54:03.550 --> 01:54:06.690
But within the group of patients who are in
hospital for more than three weeks, a very

01:54:06.690 --> 01:54:12.120
large number of them shouldn’t be in hospital;
they need care in another setting, a more

01:54:12.120 --> 01:54:13.590
appropriate setting.

01:54:13.590 --> 01:54:18.810
If we reduce the number of patients who are
in hospital for over three weeks by 25%, we

01:54:18.810 --> 01:54:23.120
would reduce – we would release 4,000 beds
in the NHS.

01:54:23.120 --> 01:54:31.540
So, we – through Pauline Philip’s leadership,
we will be launching and leading a national

01:54:31.540 --> 01:54:37.030
effort to create better flow through our hospitals,
enable patients who are inappropriately in

01:54:37.030 --> 01:54:42.760
hospital to be discharged, and at the same
time, release the capacity we need in order

01:54:42.760 --> 01:54:45.429
to get people in for operations and stop them
waiting in A&amp;E departments.

01:54:45.429 --> 01:54:51.219
So, this is a win all round, but it will require
the NHS to be more effective, be more effective

01:54:51.219 --> 01:54:59.260
in its bed management, be more cognisant of
the fact that twice as many people are discharged

01:54:59.260 --> 01:55:03.360
on the average working day, Monday through
to Friday, than are discharged on a Saturday

01:55:03.360 --> 01:55:04.360
and Sunday.

01:55:04.360 --> 01:55:06.780
How does the NHS become truly seven days a
week?

01:55:06.780 --> 01:55:08.800
And we will need support from our partners.

01:55:08.800 --> 01:55:15.219
We will need primary care to finish the roll-out
of access seven days a week, we will need

01:55:15.219 --> 01:55:20.230
nursing homes and care homes to be able to
accept patients on Saturdays and Sundays out

01:55:20.230 --> 01:55:26.310
of hospitals, we will need local authorities
to ensure that we can get patient assessments

01:55:26.310 --> 01:55:30.880
done on a Saturday and Sunday, and we will
need the community health services to be equally

01:55:30.880 --> 01:55:36.150
rigorous about being seven-day services for
discharging patients.

01:55:36.150 --> 01:55:39.579
But if we can get that – and I think this
is a big challenge for the NHS that will run

01:55:39.579 --> 01:55:44.850
for a number of years – but if we can impact
on that in the coming year, we can get patients

01:55:44.850 --> 01:55:48.909
to the right location that is more appropriate
for them, and we can create an NHS that is

01:55:48.909 --> 01:55:50.239
able to cope with the demand.

01:55:50.239 --> 01:55:55.409
So, I think the next six months of the NHS
is head down on, how do we make sure that

01:55:55.409 --> 01:56:01.050
flow runs really well in order that we can
meet our ambitions for the coming year?

01:56:01.050 --> 01:56:07.739
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Matthew, thank you.

01:56:07.739 --> 01:56:14.440
Questions, comments, from my colleagues?

01:56:14.440 --> 01:56:18.570
We’ll start with Joanne.

01:56:18.570 --> 01:56:29.730
[BREAK IN AUDIO]
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: – face-to-face care.

01:56:29.730 --> 01:56:33.490
So, I think that’s something we need to
–

01:56:33.490 --> 01:56:45.060
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah, no, that’s absolutely
a priority.

01:56:45.060 --> 01:57:09.520
And 

01:57:09.520 --> 01:57:19.890
we have recently invested some money into
Surrey University’s primary care surveillance

01:57:19.890 --> 01:57:25.480
centre, which is – belongs to the Royal
College of GPs, in order to get the team down

01:57:25.480 --> 01:57:30.110
here – there, who currently produce things
like the flu surveillance data, and gather

01:57:30.110 --> 01:57:38.790
data from GP practices, clean it, and do high-quality
analysis on it, to help us understand who

01:57:38.790 --> 01:57:43.560
is coming to see a GP that technology might
help us intervene with in order to provide

01:57:43.560 --> 01:57:46.260
a different sort of care, amongst other things.

01:57:46.260 --> 01:57:51.960
We’re – they’re working with the integrated
care system down there to do wider population

01:57:51.960 --> 01:57:56.429
health reviews, but part of what I want to
add to that is, can we get a clearer steer

01:57:56.429 --> 01:58:01.840
on the things that are impacting on primary
care, that technology could step in front

01:58:01.840 --> 01:58:07.690
of and enable people to receive – to not
need to go and see their GP, creating the

01:58:07.690 --> 01:58:12.030
space in GPs for people not to need to go
to an A&amp;E, and so on, to create that cascade

01:58:12.030 --> 01:58:13.409
effect.

01:58:13.409 --> 01:58:17.310
We now see parts of the country that are using
a digital-first approach.

01:58:17.310 --> 01:58:23.159
I was down in Sidcup last week where their
urgent treatment centre, the first thing you

01:58:23.159 --> 01:58:24.670
do is go and register on an iPad.

01:58:24.670 --> 01:58:29.270
But they encourage you to go online before
you go to the urgent treatment centre, and

01:58:29.270 --> 01:58:34.310
they think 60% to 80% of patients can be dealt
with either using the technology or through

01:58:34.310 --> 01:58:38.550
a phone call, before they – without needing
to actually turn up and see a clinician.

01:58:38.550 --> 01:58:40.349
It’s better for the patient and better for
the NHS.

01:58:40.349 --> 01:58:41.349
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Ian?

01:58:41.349 --> 01:58:45.780
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, just – so, Joanne, you
asked about self-care in relation to respiratory.

01:58:45.780 --> 01:58:56.280
And as part of our innovation and technology
tariff, we backed an innovation called myCOPD,

01:58:56.280 --> 01:59:03.200
which is basically a digital pulmonary rehab
application.

01:59:03.200 --> 01:59:09.540
And we’ve got from zero users of that to
over 50,000 in just a matter of months, so

01:59:09.540 --> 01:59:16.710
it’s a really nice example of pace of roll-out
as well as helping with winter.

01:59:16.710 --> 01:59:18.949
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: That’s impressive.

01:59:18.949 --> 01:59:19.949
Thank you for that.

01:59:19.949 --> 01:59:21.140
Matthew, thank you so much for the report.

01:59:21.140 --> 01:59:22.660
We note it, and are grateful for the update.

01:59:22.660 --> 01:59:28.300
Like all the papers today, it’s been a solid
and well-planned paper, but of course, like

01:59:28.300 --> 01:59:39.070
all the papers today, it is part of a build-up
to the year-end financial report, which Paul

01:59:39.070 --> 01:59:40.290
is about to present.

01:59:40.290 --> 01:59:42.969
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Gosh, with a build-up like
that…

01:59:42.969 --> 01:59:47.480
This is just in any case going to be a very
short update on this occasion, for three reasons,

01:59:47.480 --> 01:59:51.119
really, one of which is that we spent a lot
of time at the last meeting reflecting on

01:59:51.119 --> 01:59:54.710
where we were, why we were where we were,
and how that was all going to pan out.

01:59:54.710 --> 01:59:59.550
And there have been no real surprises since
the March discussion that we had.

01:59:59.550 --> 02:00:04.179
Secondly, for those of you who are excited
by all of this stuff, there is the prospect

02:00:04.179 --> 02:00:08.590
of the annual report and accounts, which will
be out in a few weeks’ time, and will spell

02:00:08.590 --> 02:00:13.650
out in gory detail all of the detail that
I might otherwise share with you on finance,

02:00:13.650 --> 02:00:16.690
and I have to save a few surprises for that.

02:00:16.690 --> 02:00:21.810
And thirdly, if you’re thinking of this
in terms of how it sets us up for ‘18/19,

02:00:21.810 --> 02:00:26.469
that’s a discussion to be had this afternoon,
so I won’t, again, steal my own thunder

02:00:26.469 --> 02:00:27.469
for that.

02:00:27.469 --> 02:00:28.950
So, very briefly.

02:00:28.950 --> 02:00:32.170
The year-end process is going well.

02:00:32.170 --> 02:00:37.790
Those of you who were at the Audit Committee
recently will know that the NAO is happy with

02:00:37.790 --> 02:00:43.639
the delivery that they’ve had of 200-and-something
sets of accounts from around the country of

02:00:43.639 --> 02:00:49.150
a high quality, which they are in the process
of, with their local auditors, going through.

02:00:49.150 --> 02:00:51.720
Very complimentary about the submissions that
we got.

02:00:51.720 --> 02:00:54.440
So, I think the process is going fine.

02:00:54.440 --> 02:01:00.050
In terms of the numbers, at our last meeting,
I said I rather hoped to improve on the £174

02:01:00.050 --> 02:01:05.260
million of overall underspend that we were
showing at that point, as well, of course,

02:01:05.260 --> 02:01:12.639
as delivering in full on the commitment that
we made to save a system risk reserve of £560

02:01:12.639 --> 02:01:17.619
million, and on top of that, to set aside
the £80 million that we received in terms

02:01:17.619 --> 02:01:24.080
of retrospective rebates on community pharmacy
margins, and which we release to CCGs at the

02:01:24.080 --> 02:01:25.080
year-end.

02:01:25.080 --> 02:01:28.770
And you can see the actual outcome is set
out at table 1 of my report.

02:01:28.770 --> 02:01:36.270
I’m pleased to say that, as they did in
‘16/17, CCGs and NHS England did fully deliver

02:01:36.270 --> 02:01:40.949
on the reserves that they were committed to
set aside.

02:01:40.949 --> 02:01:46.570
And in the event, the underspend, excluding
those reserves, ended up at £315 million

02:01:46.570 --> 02:01:51.250
in the draft accounts: a little bit better
than £174 million that we discussed at the

02:01:51.250 --> 02:01:52.940
last meeting.

02:01:52.940 --> 02:01:56.250
Action that we took earlier in the year meant
that some of the outstanding risks for which

02:01:56.250 --> 02:02:01.140
we had made provision through central cost
savings didn’t materialise in full, and

02:02:01.140 --> 02:02:05.699
as we also see in the more detailed tables
towards the back, the technical aspects of

02:02:05.699 --> 02:02:10.809
conversion from normal accounting to treasury
accounting, if I can put it that way, swung

02:02:10.809 --> 02:02:15.099
slightly in our favour at the year-end through
the accounts process.

02:02:15.099 --> 02:02:18.389
So, those are the numbers as they will be
going through.

02:02:18.389 --> 02:02:22.369
For completeness, I should also say that the
draft account surplus of £315 million that

02:02:22.369 --> 02:02:27.260
you see in this report – £955 million if
you include the risk reserves – will rise

02:02:27.260 --> 02:02:32.800
by a further £15 million in final accounts
due to some technical adjustments in a handful

02:02:32.800 --> 02:02:34.920
of CCGs’ accounts.

02:02:34.920 --> 02:02:38.520
So, beyond all of that, I wasn’t really
going to say much more at this point, other

02:02:38.520 --> 02:02:44.639
than expressing my gratitude to CCG, CSU and,
indeed, our own NHS England finance teams,

02:02:44.639 --> 02:02:50.239
for once again delivering a high-quality set
of accounts on time, in full, and in particular,

02:02:50.239 --> 02:02:53.920
for the professional discipline they’ve
shown in delivering the risk reserve at the

02:02:53.920 --> 02:02:59.320
end of a particularly challenging year, clearly
not just for CCGs and NHS England, but for

02:02:59.320 --> 02:03:00.719
the NHS as a whole.

02:03:00.719 --> 02:03:02.969
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, thank you very much,
Paul.

02:03:02.969 --> 02:03:08.290
And we should reflect what an extraordinary
achievement it is to land £110 billion with

02:03:08.290 --> 02:03:13.340
such fine margins at the end, and that’s
down to, as you say, the hard work across

02:03:13.340 --> 02:03:18.219
the CCGs and elsewhere, but also, when it
comes to your own team, for which we’re

02:03:18.219 --> 02:03:19.219
immensely grateful.

02:03:19.219 --> 02:03:23.520
Can I just ask whether people have any questions
or comments to Paul?

02:03:23.520 --> 02:03:24.520
No.

02:03:24.520 --> 02:03:29.750
Well, please can we just record our gratitude
to Paul for another year, and many more of

02:03:29.750 --> 02:03:30.750
the same.

02:03:30.750 --> 02:03:33.800
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Well, no promises.

02:03:33.800 --> 02:03:35.800
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good.

02:03:35.800 --> 02:03:40.730
Now may we turn to item ten, which is the
decision of the High Court on the judicial

02:03:40.730 --> 02:03:41.730
review.

02:03:41.730 --> 02:03:43.170
Ian, are you going to say anything about this?

02:03:43.170 --> 02:03:44.170
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: It’s simply for noting.

02:03:44.170 --> 02:03:45.170
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: It’s simply for noting.

02:03:45.170 --> 02:03:50.449
I think the critical thing is, this is a very
important upholding of the argument that a

02:03:50.449 --> 02:03:56.930
whole-population annual payment falls within
the legal powers of our legislation.

02:03:56.930 --> 02:04:03.949
And it is, I think, a further block in the
foundation through which we approach integrated

02:04:03.949 --> 02:04:05.170
care systems for the future.

02:04:05.170 --> 02:04:09.180
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: And just to note that we’re
on day two of the second JR today.

02:04:09.180 --> 02:04:10.440
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes.

02:04:10.440 --> 02:04:13.060
Good, thank you very much.

02:04:13.060 --> 02:04:16.670
We have, then, reports from Board committees.

02:04:16.670 --> 02:04:18.370
First from the Commissioning Committee.

02:04:18.370 --> 02:04:19.370
David?

02:04:19.370 --> 02:04:26.619
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, this goes back to Wednesday
the 28th of March, so a couple of months ago.

02:04:26.619 --> 02:04:29.119
Three things to note, if I may.

02:04:29.119 --> 02:04:35.690
First is, we do need formal ratification by
this Board of the amended terms of reference.

02:04:35.690 --> 02:04:42.239
They are merely catching up and tidying up
and consistency across various committees,

02:04:42.239 --> 02:04:46.270
and with the Board itself.

02:04:46.270 --> 02:04:50.270
But if anyone has any questions, otherwise,
we do –

02:04:50.270 --> 02:04:52.610
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Are we happy to ratify the
amended terms of reference?

02:04:52.610 --> 02:04:54.400
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thank you.

02:04:54.400 --> 02:04:59.079
Second thing is – and it was touched on
earlier – this Committee spent a considerable

02:04:59.079 --> 02:05:07.329
amount of time on primary care delivery, and
in particular, the challenges of recruitment

02:05:07.329 --> 02:05:15.510
and retention of GPs, what the management
team are doing, what the risks and opportunities

02:05:15.510 --> 02:05:24.730
were, and clearly, the – a recognition of
the tremendous stress and workload pressures

02:05:24.730 --> 02:05:28.409
on our GP colleagues around the country.

02:05:28.409 --> 02:05:32.670
I think just to highlight – I think the
Board knows it, but in all of our conversations,

02:05:32.670 --> 02:05:39.250
I think this is one of the most important
activities, and we need to come back to it.

02:05:39.250 --> 02:05:42.540
It is not without its challenges.

02:05:42.540 --> 02:05:51.050
There are a significant programme of work
going on within NHS England, but it’s also

02:05:51.050 --> 02:05:56.219
not without its risk, and therefore, I think,
Malcolm, it should be – and will be, I know

02:05:56.219 --> 02:05:58.960
– a subject ongoing.

02:05:58.960 --> 02:06:02.820
And then the final thing I’d just say is
– you stole my thunder.

02:06:02.820 --> 02:06:08.210
Just to say that we went through, as we do
every meeting, the monthly finance reviews,

02:06:08.210 --> 02:06:14.630
and I think Paul and the team, supported by
the wider NHS England team, the convenors

02:06:14.630 --> 02:06:19.370
did an astonishingly good job at managing
the finances in very difficult times.

02:06:19.370 --> 02:06:20.889
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

02:06:20.889 --> 02:06:24.760
Wendy, there’s also a Committee that you
chair on the 25th of April?

02:06:24.760 --> 02:06:25.760
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Yes.

02:06:25.760 --> 02:06:29.079
The Commissioning Committee meets ten times
a year, as you know, and we go through a range

02:06:29.079 --> 02:06:30.079
of operational topics.

02:06:30.079 --> 02:06:35.329
We spent quite a lot of time at the April
meeting particularly reviewing the statistics

02:06:35.329 --> 02:06:37.369
that Matthew was just sharing.

02:06:37.369 --> 02:06:42.809
One thing I would like to call out to the
Board’s attention is, at this meeting, we

02:06:42.809 --> 02:06:48.429
did a very extensive review of the new care
models and vanguard progress, as that programme

02:06:48.429 --> 02:06:51.260
gets morphed into the ICO and ICS programmes.

02:06:51.260 --> 02:06:58.070
And we’re incredibly encouraged by very
strong results, both on A&amp;E admission reduction,

02:06:58.070 --> 02:07:01.730
as well as overall cost improvement, and general
quality improvements.

02:07:01.730 --> 02:07:06.290
So, the Committee now is focused on saying,
‘How do we roll that into the ICOs and ICSs,

02:07:06.290 --> 02:07:08.699
going forward, to make sure we capture those
learnings?’

02:07:08.699 --> 02:07:11.219
But it was really encouraging.

02:07:11.219 --> 02:07:16.900
One thing I did want to note to the Board
is, the thing that made the difference, besides

02:07:16.900 --> 02:07:22.730
having, you know, very strong programmes,
was the leadership, was the relationships,

02:07:22.730 --> 02:07:27.300
and it was really the sense of having an overall
learning organisation made such a difference

02:07:27.300 --> 02:07:29.860
in making these programmes work and be effective.

02:07:29.860 --> 02:07:32.210
So, I hope we take that forward in other settings.

02:07:32.210 --> 02:07:33.909
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thanks a lot.

02:07:33.909 --> 02:07:38.500
And of course, there was a meeting yesterday
which will report to the next Board meeting.

02:07:38.500 --> 02:07:39.500
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Yes.

02:07:39.500 --> 02:07:44.400
And can I also report that the Finance Committee
continued to do an outstanding job in April,

02:07:44.400 --> 02:07:45.560
as well.

02:07:45.560 --> 02:07:47.290
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right.

02:07:47.290 --> 02:07:49.020
Right, very good.

02:07:49.020 --> 02:07:50.510
Investment Committee, Moira, we have a written
report.

02:07:50.510 --> 02:07:51.510
Anything to add?

02:07:51.510 --> 02:07:53.270
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: I don’t think there’s anything
to add, Malcolm.

02:07:53.270 --> 02:07:55.530
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you very much.

02:07:55.530 --> 02:07:56.710
Specialised Commissioning, Noel?

02:07:56.710 --> 02:08:02.960
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Can I also just say – begin
by saying thank you very much to John Burn,

02:08:02.960 --> 02:08:08.969
who has served on the Specialised Commissioning
Committee, has been my tutor on many occasions

02:08:08.969 --> 02:08:14.449
in an area of extraordinary complexity, and
the nature of things that we deal with in

02:08:14.449 --> 02:08:20.909
Specialised really required John’s extraordinary
clinical input, so I wanted to thank him personally

02:08:20.909 --> 02:08:23.860
for all he’s done in Specialised.

02:08:23.860 --> 02:08:29.680
We do deal with a number of really highly
complex situations in commissioning our Specialised

02:08:29.680 --> 02:08:35.820
Services, not least two that have been brought
into the aegis of Specialised Commissioning

02:08:35.820 --> 02:08:41.989
recently, Malcolm, which is our services for
armed forces and our services for health and

02:08:41.989 --> 02:08:47.590
justice, on which we spend £63 million and
£565 million respectively.

02:08:47.590 --> 02:08:58.190
It’s quite right that we give them new attention
and new focus, and under the mandate of Spec

02:08:58.190 --> 02:09:04.500
Com, we’ll be looking again at how we want
to refresh our strategy for both sectors.

02:09:04.500 --> 02:09:12.250
We’re also taking up a new field of activity,
which is looking at how we decommission services.

02:09:12.250 --> 02:09:19.090
So, it’s quite right as a learning organisation
that we do in fact recognise that some of

02:09:19.090 --> 02:09:23.560
the practices and procedures and clinical
interventions we’ve used in the past may

02:09:23.560 --> 02:09:30.989
well become obsolete over time, and are superseded
by new evidence that arises, and we’re taking

02:09:30.989 --> 02:09:37.320
those into account in the beginning of how
we develop an approach to decommissioning,

02:09:37.320 --> 02:09:44.929
which will also help us with releasing energy
and funding for other newer services.

02:09:44.929 --> 02:09:50.270
So, I wanted – those are mentioned – most
of those points are mentioned in the paper,

02:09:50.270 --> 02:09:55.120
but I just wanted to highlight those, because
they’re important pivot points for Specialised.

02:09:55.120 --> 02:09:58.889
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, thank you very much,
Noel, for that report.

02:09:58.889 --> 02:10:02.670
Any questions or comments on any of the committee
reports?

02:10:02.670 --> 02:10:03.670
No?

02:10:03.670 --> 02:10:07.510
May we note them all, and we’ve already
agreed to ratify the changes in the terms

02:10:07.510 --> 02:10:10.099
of reference for the Commissioning Committee.

02:10:10.099 --> 02:10:12.570
That brings us almost to the end of the agenda.

02:10:12.570 --> 02:10:16.940
Before I invite the Board to pass the usual
resolution of going into private business,

02:10:16.940 --> 02:10:20.150
can I just raise one or two items of any other
business?

02:10:20.150 --> 02:10:25.760
The first is to report formally to the Board
the announcement which has been put out this

02:10:25.760 --> 02:10:32.020
morning while we were on air, which is of
Jane’s intention to retire as Chief Nursing

02:10:32.020 --> 02:10:33.020
Officer.

02:10:33.020 --> 02:10:37.429
I’m happy to say it’s not immediate; it
gives us notice which will take us through

02:10:37.429 --> 02:10:38.429
over the next six months.

02:10:38.429 --> 02:10:44.380
But I knew that the Board, because it is a
Board matter, would wish to be informed formally

02:10:44.380 --> 02:10:45.380
of that.

02:10:45.380 --> 02:10:48.510
There will be other opportunities, Jane, for
us to commend and thank you for all the work

02:10:48.510 --> 02:10:54.060
that you’ve done, but today, I’d just
like formally to put on record that we have

02:10:54.060 --> 02:10:58.870
notice, and we will in due course be moving
to fill that post.

02:10:58.870 --> 02:10:59.870
But thank you again, Jane.

02:10:59.870 --> 02:11:00.870
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Thank you, Malcolm.

02:11:00.870 --> 02:11:07.369
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: And secondly, just finally,
to, again, thank John Burn in the last minute

02:11:07.369 --> 02:11:11.670
of his last meeting for everything you’ve
done.

02:11:11.670 --> 02:11:17.010
And Noel’s put it brilliantly in terms of
your contribution on Spec Com, but also more

02:11:17.010 --> 02:11:19.020
widely across the rest of the Board.

02:11:19.020 --> 02:11:23.679
I think everybody knows that they’ve learned
quite a lot from you, and have enjoyed very

02:11:23.679 --> 02:11:26.730
much the contribution you’ve made to our
business, so thank you, again.

02:11:26.730 --> 02:11:30.320
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Well, can I thank you, Malcolm,
because I’ve taken what I’ve learned from

02:11:30.320 --> 02:11:32.530
you back to being Chair at the hospital in
Newcastle, and it’s –

02:11:32.530 --> 02:11:37.619
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I wish it well under those
circumstances.

02:11:37.619 --> 02:11:40.889
Right.

02:11:40.889 --> 02:11:41.889
Thank you.

02:11:41.889 --> 02:11:46.170
Our next meeting’s on the 4th of July, and
that brings an end to our business today.

02:11:46.170 --> 02:11:51.079
Pass the normal resolution, but to remind
all colleagues that at 2pm, we’ll be back

02:11:51.079 --> 02:11:55.280
in this room for our joint meeting with the
Board of NHS Improvement.

02:11:55.280 --> 02:11:56.390
Thank you, all.

02:11:56.390 --> 02:11:57.899
The meeting is closed.

